/* Generated from content/case-studies.json and content/case-study-stories.json. */
window.CASE_STUDIES = {"onespace":{"slug":"onespace","name":"OneSpace","kind":"ux","headline":"Make the next class easier to find.","summary":"A research-led communication concept for students and teachers navigating classes, files, and announcements across disconnected apps.","role":"UX research, problem framing, flows & interface design","status":"Design challenge concept","context":"Microsoft Design Challenge · Education communication","facts":[{"value":"127","label":"survey responses"},{"value":"5+","label":"one-to-one interviews"},{"value":"Mobile","label":"primary prototype"}],"problem":"During the shift to online classes, students were being asked to follow different teachers across different communication tools. My starting question was whether bringing communication into one place could make collaboration easier. The more useful question became: how does a student find the right resource when they need it?","process":["I began with more than five one-to-one interviews and a virtual discussion with students from different colleges, then used a ten-question survey answered by 127 people. The interviews exposed an adoption mismatch: teachers chose tools they found comfortable, while students inherited the work of keeping up with all of them.","The survey complicated my original assumption. The deck reports that more than half of respondents did not mind using multiple apps, while about 14% found it frustrating. Interest in an all-in-one tool was therefore a signal to explore, rather than proof that every communication tool needed replacing. I translated the findings into a student persona, four problem areas, information architecture, wireframes, and high-fidelity screens."],"decisions":[{"title":"Organise around a class, then its conversations","choice":"The concept uses spaces and categorised channels, with calls accessible from the chat context.","reason":"A class provides a recognisable place to return to; students should not have to remember which external app a teacher chose.","tradeoff":"This would require institutional participation. A cleaner student interface alone cannot solve fragmented adoption."},{"title":"Give files and announcements their own home","choice":"I separated a unified file store and an announcements centre from the conversation stream.","reason":"These are the items students need to retrieve later. Their usefulness should not depend on scrolling back through chat.","tradeoff":"Separating destinations adds navigation; useful defaults and clear links back to the class would be essential."},{"title":"Treat access as part of the product scope","choice":"The prototype explores language support and preferences alongside the core communication screens.","reason":"The interviews describe uneven comfort with technology. Familiar structures and understandable language matter as much as consolidating features.","tradeoff":"The deck also explores watch support and parental controls. Today I would defer those until the core retrieval journey had been tested."}],"outcome":"I produced an end-to-end communication concept covering spaces, channels, chat, files, announcements, calls, and preferences. The clearest design result is the separation of time-sensitive announcements and retrievable resources from the conversation stream. The original project ended at prototype stage, with usability studies identified as the next step.","reflection":"My most useful takeaway is that willingness to try an all-in-one app is different from needing one. Revisiting the work, I would position the first release around finding class resources and acting on announcements, then earn the right to expand.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask students to retrieve an assignment, identify the latest announcement, and join the correct class; compare task success and recovery with their current workflow.","Proposed scope: pilot one class before adding institution-wide migration, parental controls, or a second device. Include teacher setup effort in the evaluation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 2–7","detail":"Process, problem statement, interviews, survey and problem identification."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 11–17","detail":"Information architecture, wireframes, final screens and the proposed usability study."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p05.webp","caption":"The original survey: a useful counterpoint to my initial assumption about app overload.","alt":"Original OneSpace survey slide with response charts."},{"src":"assets/cases/onespace/p14.webp","caption":"Files and announcements become explicit destinations instead of getting buried in chat.","alt":"Original OneSpace file store, announcements and profile screens."}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/onespace.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/onespace.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":3,"narrative":{"headline":"The class starts soon. [[Where is that file?]]","scene":"Picture a student getting ready for an online class. The conversation is in one app, the link in another, and the assignment somewhere further back. Before learning can begin, there is a search to finish.","question":"Would putting everything into one app actually solve the problem?","insight":"More than half of the survey respondents were [[fine with using multiple apps]]. That changed the question: the stronger opportunity was making class resources easier to retrieve.","pivot":"I organised the concept around a dependable place to return to: the class, its conversations, and the resources attached to it.","takeaway":"A useful home for information matters more than an all-in-one promise.","journey":[{"title":"Find the class","detail":"A recognisable space becomes the starting point."},{"title":"Find the context","detail":"Channels keep the conversation attached to its class."},{"title":"Retrieve what matters","detail":"Files and announcements have their own destinations."}],"decisionLeads":["First, give the student somewhere to return.","Then, design for the moment after a message is sent.","Finally, decide how much the first release should do."],"annotations":[{"title":"Files outlive the chat","detail":"A dedicated file store supports later retrieval."},{"title":"Announcements deserve a signal","detail":"Important class information gets a destination of its own."},{"title":"Preferences stay close","detail":"The prototype explores personal settings alongside the core flow."}],"evidenceTag":"Original survey · 127 responses","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Original research persona","label":"Vivek · student","context":"An engineering student attending online classes across tools chosen by different teachers.","need":"Retrieve class resources when they are needed.","friction":"Resources scattered across apps, limited phone storage, and difficulty asking for help.","implication":"Give the class and its resources a predictable home."}},"theme":{"layout":"collaboration","mode":"light","paper":"#f7fbff","ink":"#173447","dim":"#53697d","line":"#cbdfe9","accent":"#087e88","wash":"#dcf3f4","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Class confusion","process":"What the research changed","decisions":"Designing a dependable home","outcome":"The communication concept","reflection":"The next research question"},"decisionHeading":"Keep class resources easy to find.","resultHeading":"A class, with a place for its resources."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/onespace/p14.webp","alt":"Original OneSpace file store, announcements and profile screens.","caption":"The original screens give files and announcements destinations beyond the conversation stream.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/onespace.pdf","focus":[{"title":"Retrieve","detail":"Files have a searchable destination."},{"title":"Notice","detail":"Announcements have a separate reading surface."},{"title":"Personalise","detail":"Preferences remain close to the core experience."}],"width":1600,"height":900,"details":[{"decision":1,"x":95,"y":190,"width":840,"height":610,"caption":"Files and announcements each have a dedicated destination in the original prototype.","alt":"Detail of the original OneSpace file store and announcements screens."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"umatter","name":"uMatter","headline":"Lower the threshold for asking for help."}},"umatter":{"slug":"umatter","name":"uMatter","kind":"ux","headline":"Lower the threshold for asking for help.","summary":"A 14-hour team challenge exploring a more approachable route from mental-health awareness to finding and booking professional support.","role":"Team lead · UX research & design","status":"Team design challenge concept","context":"Team Elementors · Pixel Perfect UI/UX Challenge","facts":[{"value":"14h","label":"challenge window"},{"value":"65","label":"survey responses"},{"value":"3","label":"team members"}],"problem":"The challenge brief focused on making access to mental-health support more efficient. I led the UX research and design contribution for Team Elementors, working with Pratik Manjrekar on UI design and Nidhi S Kamath on UI and graphic design. Our task was to turn a broad and sensitive brief into a coherent mobile journey within fourteen hours.","process":["We used a shortened Double Diamond process to divide research, structure, and interface work. Our ten-question survey received 65 responses. The deck reports that almost half wanted professional help while remaining anonymous, and almost 89% had never visited a therapist. Those responses guided the concept toward privacy expectations and an approachable first step.","We supplemented the survey with article research, assembled a persona, mapped the flow, and progressed from wireframes to high-fidelity screens. The sample was exploratory: the deck does not document recruitment, a representative population, or a controlled evaluation. I treat its percentages as directional responses from this sample."],"decisions":[{"title":"Build a path to support, rather than promising diagnosis","choice":"The concept connects expert discovery, an expert profile, appointment selection, and payment.","reason":"Breaking access into visible steps gives a person a clearer idea of what happens after they decide to seek help.","tradeoff":"Although the challenge brief used the word diagnosis, these screens do not demonstrate clinical diagnosis or treatment effectiveness."},{"title":"Put trust information before commitment","choice":"Expert detail and review-based trust cues sit before calendar booking and payment in the documented journey.","reason":"A person needs to understand who they are approaching before being asked to commit time or money.","tradeoff":"Ratings alone do not establish professional competence. Credential verification would need its own operational process."},{"title":"Explore lower-pressure ways to engage","choice":"The team added awareness content, stories, and lighter activities alongside the booking journey.","reason":"Not every visitor is ready to book immediately. The concept offers a way to explore the topic before taking that step.","tradeoff":"More destinations can dilute the primary route to professional support; I would test whether they help or distract."}],"outcome":"Together, we completed a mobile concept spanning onboarding, expert discovery, provider details, booking, payment, and awareness content within the challenge window. I led the team and contributed UX research and design; Pratik and Nidhi contributed the UI and graphic-design work credited in the original deck. The deliverable was a research-informed prototype.","reflection":"The research surfaced an important unresolved tension: wanting anonymity and booking an accountable professional service are different requirements. Today I would define what information is visible at each step, explain its purpose, and make the service boundary explicit before expanding the feature set.","nextSteps":["Proposed validation: test whether participants understand what is private, what a provider can see, and whether the product offers booking or diagnosis.","Proposed iteration: add clear provider credentials, consent and cancellation information, then evaluate the booking journey with appropriate professional input."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 2–7","detail":"Named team roles, fourteen-hour constraint, survey size and reported responses."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 10–18","detail":"Persona, solution framing, user flow, wireframes and final booking screens."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/p02.webp","caption":"The original team slide preserves who contributed what.","alt":"Team Elementors slide naming Srinidhi Bhat, Pratik Manjrekar and Nidhi S Kamath."},{"src":"assets/cases/umatter/p17.webp","caption":"The designed route from discovering an expert to viewing details, choosing a date, and payment.","alt":"Original expert list, feed, expert profile, calendar and payment screens."}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/umatter.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/umatter.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":6,"narrative":{"headline":"Asking for help starts [[before the appointment]].","scene":"Imagine being ready to explore professional support, but unsure who will see your details or what the first step involves. Every unclear commitment can make it easier to postpone the search.","question":"What should someone understand before being asked to book?","insight":"In our 65-response survey, almost half wanted professional help while remaining anonymous. [[Privacy expectations]] had to shape the journey before calendar and payment screens.","pivot":"Within a fourteen-hour challenge, I led the UX contribution toward a visible sequence: understand the provider, choose a time, then commit.","takeaway":"Trust begins with knowing what the next step asks of you.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Start with providers and support information."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"See the expert’s details before committing."},{"title":"Book","detail":"Move through calendar selection and payment."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the service boundary clear.","Earn confidence before asking for commitment.","Leave room for someone who is still exploring."],"annotations":[{"title":"Context before commitment","detail":"Expert details sit before the calendar and payment."},{"title":"A visible sequence","detail":"The concept separates discovery, consideration, and booking."},{"title":"A team outcome","detail":"I led UX research/design; Pratik and Nidhi contributed UI and graphic design."}],"evidenceTag":"Original research · 65 responses","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Original team research persona","label":"Shritija · student seeking support","context":"The original deck describes a medical student balancing studies with a need for professional support.","need":"Understand a provider and the first commitment before booking.","friction":"Limited time and a lack of support around seeking help.","implication":"Put provider information and privacy expectations before the appointment."}},"theme":{"layout":"care","mode":"light","paper":"#faf9ff","ink":"#242444","dim":"#62627c","line":"#dedced","accent":"#5356a8","wash":"#eeedfb","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Before asking for help","process":"Listening to the sample","decisions":"Designing for consideration","outcome":"The booking journey","reflection":"What trust needs next"},"decisionHeading":"Help people decide who to book.","resultHeading":"From considering support to choosing a time."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/umatter/p17.webp","alt":"Original expert list, feed, expert profile, calendar and payment screens.","caption":"The designed route from discovering an expert to viewing details, choosing a date, and payment.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/umatter.pdf","width":1600,"height":900},"nextStory":{"slug":"mucoin","name":"MuCoin","headline":"Earn trust one financial detail at a time."}},"mucoin":{"slug":"mucoin","name":"MuCoin","kind":"ux","headline":"Earn trust one financial detail at a time.","summary":"A personal-finance onboarding concept that turns income, expenses, and savings goals into a reviewable starting picture.","role":"Product framing, onboarding flow & interface rationale","status":"Prototype & design rationale","context":"Personal finance · Progressive onboarding","facts":[{"value":"5","label":"onboarding stages"},{"value":"3","label":"review groups"},{"value":"Prototype","label":"linked in source"}],"problem":"A useful financial picture depends on personal information that takes effort to recall and can feel uncomfortable to share. I structured MuCoin around a sequence that asks for income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, savings goals, and a final review. The design challenge was to make that effort understandable and correctable.","process":["The original write-up explains the flow screen by screen, including privacy links, expense summaries, editable goals, and review behaviour. It also defines proposed KPIs for each stage. I have reorganised that material around the decisions behind the flow, rather than repeating general design laws.","The source mentions a small user subset when discussing the label Saving Goals, but gives no sample size, protocol, or measured comparison. I treat that as informal input. The numerical completion and drop-off figures in the appendix are targets, not results."],"decisions":[{"title":"Ask for a small, repeatable unit of information","choice":"The income stage starts with amount and source, while fixed and variable expenses use similar entry patterns.","reason":"A familiar pattern lets the person concentrate on their finances instead of relearning the interface at every step.","tradeoff":"A short form still needs room for irregular income, uncertain estimates, and multiple sources; simplicity cannot mean forcing inaccurate answers."},{"title":"Show a useful picture before onboarding ends","choice":"The expense stages return a chart, summary table, total, and relationship to income.","reason":"Summaries create opportunities to notice a missing entry or an implausible total while the information is still fresh.","tradeoff":"A chart is only as good as its inputs. It should communicate an estimate when the person is unsure, rather than imply financial precision."},{"title":"Make confirmation an editable checkpoint","choice":"The review groups income, expenses, and goals into three collapsible, editable sections.","reason":"Being able to inspect and correct information gives the person control over what they submit.","tradeoff":"The old rationale claims review ensures complete task success. That is a hypothesis to test; a confirmation screen cannot guarantee it."}],"outcome":"I turned a demanding setup task into a five-stage onboarding prototype with repeatable entry patterns, immediate summaries, and an editable review. The accompanying rationale defines how the flow could be measured. Its completion and drop-off figures are proposed targets rather than observed outcomes.","reflection":"I would now judge the flow by the quality of the starting financial picture and the person’s understanding of it. More time on a goal screen or more celebration triggers are not inherently better outcomes. Successful correction should count as useful behaviour, not friction to eliminate.","nextSteps":["Proposed measures: completion per stage, time to a usable summary, errors noticed during review, and understanding of what happens to entered information.","Proposed study: compare a short path with optional detail against the original sequence. Include irregular income and skipped estimates rather than testing only ideal inputs."],"sources":[{"label":"Original write-up, pp. 1–5","detail":"Project purpose and screen-by-screen design rationale."},{"label":"Original write-up, pp. 6–11","detail":"KPI definitions and aspirational targets; no measured results reported."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p02.webp","caption":"The source is a written design rationale. Its performance claims are reframed here as hypotheses.","alt":"Original MuCoin rationale for income and expense onboarding."}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/mucoin.pdf","prototypeUrl":"https://www.figma.com/proto/fTSm9Chs0awMrjMUN2OWSa/MuCoin?node-id=1-2&starting-point-node-id=191%3A955&t=z97O9d2NsQAgujdk-1","cover":"assets/work/mucoin.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":9,"narrative":{"headline":"Before the budget comes [[a lot of questions]].","scene":"Picture someone setting up a finance app. They are recalling income, estimating expenses, and deciding how much personal information to share—all before seeing whether the product is useful.","question":"How can the setup give something back before it is finished?","insight":"The flow already contained a useful idea: [[show the emerging financial picture]] while a person enters it. Each summary can become a chance to understand and correct the information.","pivot":"I structured the onboarding as a repeatable sequence with visible progress, immediate summaries, and an editable checkpoint.","takeaway":"A good onboarding flow helps someone trust the information they just entered.","journey":[{"title":"Enter","detail":"Ask for a small, repeatable unit of information."},{"title":"See","detail":"Return a summary while the context is fresh."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Make the complete picture editable before submission."}],"decisionLeads":["Start with one manageable question.","Give the input a visible consequence.","Let review become an act of control."],"annotations":[{"title":"Five connected stages","detail":"Income, fixed expenses, variable expenses, goals, and review."},{"title":"A measurement plan","detail":"The original percentages are targets for evaluation, not achieved results."}],"evidenceTag":"Original flow rationale · five stages","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person setting up a budget","context":"Preparing a first picture of income, spending, and savings.","need":"Enter information accurately and understand what it adds up to.","friction":"Recall takes effort; mistakes are easy to make before the full picture is visible.","implication":"Give input an immediate summary and an editable review."}},"theme":{"layout":"ledger","mode":"light","paper":"#f4f8ff","ink":"#142d57","dim":"#536782","line":"#d0dded","accent":"#205bb7","wash":"#e1edff","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"The cost of setup","process":"A picture worth reviewing","decisions":"Five stages, one pattern","outcome":"The proposed onboarding","reflection":"Measuring understanding"},"decisionHeading":"Make each onboarding step useful.","resultHeading":"A financial picture that can be corrected."},"visual":{"presentation":"document","src":"assets/cases/mucoin/p02.webp","alt":"Original MuCoin rationale for income and expense onboarding.","caption":"The source is a written design rationale. Its performance claims are reframed here as hypotheses.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/mucoin.pdf","width":1120,"height":1585},"nextStory":{"slug":"gmd","name":"GetMetaData","headline":"Turn a dense SEO readout into a usable inspection tool."}},"gmd":{"slug":"gmd","name":"GetMetaData","kind":"ux","headline":"Turn a dense SEO readout into a usable inspection tool.","summary":"A browser-extension redesign exploring information hierarchy for people with different levels of SEO experience.","role":"Competitive review, information architecture, wireframes & UI","status":"Redesign prototype","context":"Chrome extension · SEO inspection","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"competitors reviewed"},{"value":"1","label":"shared comparison site"},{"value":"2","label":"persona perspectives"}],"problem":"GetMetaData exposes a page’s SEO elements, but a dense readout makes it difficult to see what needs attention. The original review describes small text, weak information architecture, and a repeated-output bug. My task was to improve inspection without stripping away the useful detail or export capability.","process":["I compared SEO META in 1 CLICK, All in One SEO Analyzer, and Detailed SEO Extension alongside GetMetaData. I used my own portfolio as the common test page and looked at functionality, usability, SEO information, and documentation. This kept the content context consistent, although one website could not represent every page structure.","I then used two persona perspectives—an SEO engineer and a small-business/content-marketing user—to frame different reading needs, before creating wireframes and a high-fidelity prototype. The deck does not document interviews validating those personas, so I use them as design lenses rather than research findings."],"decisions":[{"title":"Retain information depth, improve the route through it","choice":"The redesign explores a clearer hierarchy for metadata, headings, images, structured data, and links.","reason":"An inspector should help a person locate relevant evidence before asking them to interpret a full page of metrics.","tradeoff":"A more segmented interface can make cross-category comparison harder. Testing should include jumping between related issues."},{"title":"Compare tools in a controlled content context","choice":"I reviewed the competing extensions against the same portfolio page.","reason":"Using one common page makes differences in presentation easier to discuss without changing the underlying website at every comparison.","tradeoff":"The review is a historical snapshot on one site, not a current market ranking or a large performance benchmark."},{"title":"Separate interface redesign from a functional repair","choice":"The review records duplicated output as a bug, while the deliverable remains an interface prototype.","reason":"Making data easier to read would not fix incorrect or repeated extraction. The two problems need separate acceptance criteria.","tradeoff":"The prototype can demonstrate hierarchy and navigation; it cannot prove the extraction bug was resolved."}],"outcome":"I delivered a competitive review, persona framing, wireframes, and a linked high-fidelity prototype. The work makes the information-design problem more specific: preserve useful depth and export, improve the route to relevant information, and keep extraction correctness separate from visual presentation. The completed scope is a redesign prototype.","reflection":"This project sharpened the distinction between exposing more data and making the data useful. Today I would organise the evaluation around actual inspection tasks and preserve export as part of the workflow, rather than judge success from visual freshness alone.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a novice and an experienced practitioner to locate a missing title, inspect image descriptions, and export their findings.","Proposed acceptance criteria: correct extraction without duplication, clear reading order, and useful handling of a page with unusually long or missing values."],"sources":[{"label":"Original deck, pp. 3–9","detail":"Problem, comparison method, historical tool observations and comparison mapping."},{"label":"Original deck, pp. 10–14","detail":"Persona perspectives, wireframes and final prototype."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/p08.webp","caption":"The original review separates useful capabilities from readability and data-quality problems.","alt":"Original GetMetaData strengths and weaknesses slide."},{"src":"assets/cases/gmd/p14.webp","caption":"The final prototype provides the visual outcome; task-based validation remains the next step.","alt":"Original high-fidelity GetMetaData extension design."}],"featured":true,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/gmd.pdf","prototypeUrl":"https://www.figma.com/proto/P86VsJJUoQDMzxsUFCnRHo/3MMaven-Project?node-id=8-2&starting-point-node-id=8%3A2&t=K4zjIkNOs7p8WSiR-1","cover":"assets/work/gmd.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":12,"narrative":{"headline":"All the metadata. [[Which part needs attention?]]","scene":"Imagine opening an SEO extension to answer one question about a page. The tool has plenty of data, but finding the relevant item becomes another inspection task.","question":"What should stay visible when an inspector becomes easier to read?","insight":"The historical review found [[useful depth and export]] alongside small text, weak organisation, and duplicated output. A visual refresh needed to preserve the valuable workflow.","pivot":"I separated three concerns: finding information, interpreting it, and making sure the extraction itself is correct.","takeaway":"Information depth becomes useful when there is a clear route through it.","journey":[{"title":"Locate","detail":"Get to the relevant category."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Read the page evidence in context."},{"title":"Carry forward","detail":"Keep export useful in the wider workflow."}],"decisionLeads":["Give the data a reading order.","Hold the comparison context steady.","Keep correctness on its own checklist."],"annotations":[{"title":"Grouped information","detail":"The high-fidelity prototype explores a clearer inspection hierarchy."},{"title":"A usable work surface","detail":"Readability and navigation are part of the tool’s utility."},{"title":"A separate acceptance criterion","detail":"The repeated-output bug needs an product behaviour fix beyond this prototype."}],"evidenceTag":"Historical review · three competitors","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Original persona framing · not interview-validated","label":"SEO specialist & small-business content owner","context":"Two personas in the original deck frame expert inspection and a less technical reading need.","need":"Find and understand relevant page information.","friction":"Dense lists make scanning difficult; unfamiliar terms make the same display harder for a novice.","implication":"Preserve depth while creating clearer groups and entry points."}},"theme":{"layout":"inspector","mode":"light","paper":"#f6faff","ink":"#203146","dim":"#516984","line":"#cbdbee","accent":"#286cad","wash":"#e4effc","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A dense readout","process":"Two ways of reading","decisions":"Making inspection clearer","outcome":"The redesigned extension","reflection":"Testing the hierarchy"},"decisionHeading":"Help different users scan the same metadata.","resultHeading":"A more structured inspection surface."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/gmd/p14.webp","alt":"Original high-fidelity GetMetaData extension design.","caption":"The final prototype provides the visual outcome; task-based validation remains the next step.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/gmd.pdf","width":1600,"height":900},"nextStory":{"slug":"steve-wilson","name":"Steve Wilson","headline":"Give a designer’s work a clear reading order."}},"steve-wilson":{"slug":"steve-wilson","name":"Steve Wilson","kind":"ux","headline":"Give a designer’s work a clear reading order.","summary":"An early portfolio concept exploring typography, project browsing, and a repeatable case-study template.","role":"Visual direction, page structure & interface concept","status":"Independent visual concept","context":"Designer portfolio · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"page types"},{"value":"Type-led","label":"visual direction"},{"value":"Concept","label":"not a client engagement"}],"problem":"A portfolio has to communicate what a designer does, offer a convincing sample of their work, and make contact straightforward. This early concept uses the fictional Steve Wilson identity to explore that sequence through a home page, work listing, project-detail template, and contact destination.","process":["The available evidence is a four-page visual artifact, including a long project-detail template. It contains sample copy, mock project names, placeholder results, and a sample client attribution. Those are layout content, not my client history or achieved results.","For this retrospective I evaluated the visible structure: the introduction establishes the designer’s role, the work listing exposes project categories and tools, and the detail template reserves space for the problem, process, outcome, and lessons. No original research or testing record accompanies it."],"decisions":[{"title":"Use typography to establish the first impression","choice":"The concept combines a large name treatment with a restrained dark background and a bright accent.","reason":"The visual hierarchy gives the identity a clear point of entry before visitors scan services or projects.","tradeoff":"A strong name treatment cannot replace useful positioning copy; I would make the designer’s particular value more specific."},{"title":"Separate browsing from deep reading","choice":"The work listing acts as an index, while project details have a longer narrative structure.","reason":"Visitors can skim the portfolio before deciding which story deserves more attention.","tradeoff":"A listing only helps if its labels explain the work rather than relying on unfamiliar project names."},{"title":"Give each project the same narrative spine","choice":"The template includes role, problem, process, results, takeaways, and final product.","reason":"A repeated structure helps a reviewer compare contributions across projects.","tradeoff":"In the archive, much of that structure is empty sample content. A template is useful scaffolding, not evidence of a completed case study."}],"outcome":"I produced a visual portfolio concept with a work index, reusable project-detail structure, and contact destination. It demonstrates an early attempt to make a portfolio readable at both scanning and deep-reading levels. The fictional identity, sample client attribution, and numerical placeholders belong to the template, not my professional record.","reflection":"The retrospective lesson is directly relevant to my portfolio today: a convincing case study needs specific decisions and defensible evidence inside the layout. Attractive headings cannot do that work on their own.","nextSteps":["Proposed iteration: replace the sample project with one real story, then test whether a reviewer can identify the role, central decision, and outcome in a short scan.","Proposed UI review: verify contrast, keyboard navigation, responsive reading order, and a concise contact path before implementation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, pp. 1–4","detail":"Home, work list, project-detail template and contact; sample identities and results are concept content."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p01.webp","caption":"Original home-page concept: identity, introduction, and services.","alt":"Dark Steve Wilson home-page concept with a yellow accent."},{"src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p02.webp","caption":"The work list keeps project browsing separate from the longer case-study template.","alt":"Original Steve Wilson project listing."}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/steve-wilson.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/swportfolio.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":15,"narrative":{"headline":"A portfolio earns [[a second minute]].","scene":"Picture a reviewer opening a designer’s portfolio between other applications. The first page has to establish what the designer does and make one relevant project easy to choose.","question":"What makes someone want to move from a first impression into the work?","insight":"The original concept already separates [[browsing from deep reading]]. Its project template creates room for a story, but that room still needs real decisions and evidence.","pivot":"I used the retrospective to connect the visual hierarchy to the reviewer’s sequence: orient, select, investigate, then make contact.","takeaway":"The layout earns attention; the evidence earns confidence.","journey":[{"title":"Orient","detail":"Understand the designer’s role."},{"title":"Choose","detail":"Scan a focused work index."},{"title":"Investigate","detail":"Open a project with room for contribution and process."}],"decisionLeads":["Establish a clear first impression.","Let browsing remain lightweight.","Give the detail page something real to say."],"annotations":[{"title":"A type-led opening","detail":"The name treatment establishes the visual entry point."},{"title":"A separate work index","detail":"Projects can be scanned before opening the longer story."}],"evidenceTag":"Original visual concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Prospective client reviewing a portfolio","context":"Scanning for relevant work before deciding whether to make contact.","need":"Understand the designer’s offer and inspect a suitable example.","friction":"A polished introduction alone does not explain the work.","implication":"Move quickly from identity to selected projects."}},"theme":{"layout":"portfolio","mode":"dark","paper":"#101010","ink":"#f5f4ed","dim":"#c3c3b8","line":"#3f3f37","accent":"#f1d548","wash":"#343019","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A first impression","process":"The useful signal","decisions":"Identity into evidence","outcome":"The portfolio concept","reflection":"Adding project depth"},"decisionHeading":"Lead from the work into the case study.","resultHeading":"A strong front door, with depth still to add."},"visual":{"presentation":"split","src":"assets/cases/steve-wilson/p02.webp","alt":"Original Steve Wilson project listing.","caption":"The work list keeps project browsing separate from the longer case-study template.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/steve-wilson.pdf","width":1600,"height":1800},"nextStory":{"slug":"gloria-furniture","name":"Gloria Furniture","headline":"Help a shopper move from inspiration to a shortlist."}},"gloria-furniture":{"slug":"gloria-furniture","name":"Gloria Furniture","kind":"ux","headline":"Help a shopper move from inspiration to a shortlist.","summary":"A furniture storefront concept connecting room-led browsing, product comparison, and purchase actions.","role":"Visual design & storefront information hierarchy","status":"Storefront concept","context":"E-commerce · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"featured product groups"},{"value":"Category-led","label":"browsing"},{"value":"Concept","label":"no sales results recorded"}],"problem":"Furniture is both a visual and a practical purchase. The original Gloria concept tries to support inspiration at the top of the page, category exploration in the middle, and concrete products with prices further down. The design question is how to make those modes feel like one useful shopping path.","process":["The surviving PDF is a single long storefront design, not a research report. I reviewed its hero, category imagery, quality messaging, product cards, and service links to make the underlying structure explicit. The archived dollar prices, warranty copy, and contact details are sample storefront content, not verified business facts.","This is a retrospective reading of the design. The available artifact does not establish customer interviews, a checkout implementation, or commercial performance."],"decisions":[{"title":"Start with inspiration, then offer more specific choices","choice":"The hero leads into visual categories before the featured Home Decor and Furniture groups.","reason":"A shopper can begin with a broad interest and progressively narrow to a product, instead of meeting a full catalogue immediately.","tradeoff":"The path would need descriptive category labels and accessible images; atmosphere alone does not tell a shopper where to go."},{"title":"Make product cards comparable","choice":"The featured groups repeat name, price, image, Add to Cart, and Buy Now treatments.","reason":"A stable card structure supports scanning across products and makes purchase actions predictable.","tradeoff":"Two equally prominent purchase actions can compete. Today I would test a primary product-detail path before offering immediate purchase everywhere."},{"title":"Keep practical reassurance near the shopping journey","choice":"The concept includes a quality band and footer links for stores, warranty claims, and custom requests.","reason":"Furniture decisions often require service and fulfilment information as well as appealing photography.","tradeoff":"Those claims need real policies and product information. The visual concept alone cannot establish quality or a working support service."}],"outcome":"I produced a complete storefront landing-page concept with category-led browsing and reusable product cards. The page connects an aspirational introduction to practical comparison and service information. Its contribution is visual merchandising and information hierarchy at concept stage.","reflection":"The next step is less about adding decoration and more about helping a person decide: dimensions, materials, delivery area, and stock status would make this design a more credible shopping tool. I would prioritise that information before increasing promotional content.","nextSteps":["Proposed task: find a product within a budget, compare two options, and explain delivery expectations. Record missing information as well as completion.","Proposed iteration: build a product-detail page and a narrow-screen catalogue, using real product data and one clearly prioritised action."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Full storefront: hero, category browsing, quality messages, featured groups and service footer."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_01.webp","caption":"The original hierarchy moves from an aspirational hero to category-led browsing.","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture hero and category layout."},{"src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_02.webp","caption":"Repeated product cards carry the browsing pattern through to purchase actions.","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture featured product groups."}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/gloria-furniture.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/gloriafurniture.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":18,"narrative":{"headline":"Inspiration gets a glance. [[Details make a shortlist]].","scene":"Imagine finding a piece of furniture you like, then asking whether it fits the room, the budget, and the practicalities of buying it. A storefront has to support both kinds of attention.","question":"How does visual browsing become a useful buying decision?","insight":"The concept moves from [[inspiration to categories to comparable products]]. That sequence is the useful foundation; dimensions and fulfilment would make the next step stronger.","pivot":"I organised the retrospective around how the page narrows a shopper’s options, then identified the information still needed to support a purchase.","takeaway":"A beautiful product card should help someone decide what to do next.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Start from an image or a category."},{"title":"Compare","detail":"Use a repeated card pattern to scan options."},{"title":"Decide","detail":"Bring practical product and delivery information into the next step."}],"decisionLeads":["Give inspiration a direction.","Make comparison predictable.","Connect reassurance to real information."],"annotations":[{"title":"Category-led discovery","detail":"The upper page offers ways into the catalogue."},{"title":"Repeated product cards","detail":"The lower page carries names, prices, and actions in a consistent pattern."}],"evidenceTag":"Original storefront concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Furniture shopper making a shortlist","context":"Browsing room inspiration and comparing categories.","need":"Find a relevant collection and enough detail to continue.","friction":"A visual catalogue can leave practical questions unanswered.","implication":"Connect inspiration to categories and a next product decision."}},"theme":{"layout":"catalogue","mode":"light","paper":"#fffaf8","ink":"#2b2320","dim":"#79615a","line":"#e8d9d1","accent":"#bc273a","wash":"#f9e2e2","bodyFont":"Poppins","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A room in mind","process":"Inspiration needs a route","decisions":"From room to product","outcome":"The storefront concept","reflection":"Beyond the first screen"},"decisionHeading":"Help shoppers compare a collection.","resultHeading":"A catalogue organised around the room."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/gloria-furniture/p01_02.webp","alt":"Original Gloria Furniture featured product groups.","caption":"Repeated product cards carry the browsing pattern through to purchase actions.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/gloria-furniture.pdf","width":1400,"height":1969},"nextStory":{"slug":"fun-cruises","name":"Fun Cruises Goa","headline":"Answer the questions that come before a booking."}},"fun-cruises":{"slug":"fun-cruises","name":"Fun Cruises Goa","kind":"ux","headline":"Answer the questions that come before a booking.","summary":"A yacht-rental landing-page concept organised around capacity, price, and the next enquiry.","role":"Landing-page structure, fleet browsing & visual design","status":"Website redesign concept","context":"Travel & leisure · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"fleet filter dimensions"},{"value":"Capacity","label":"visible on listings"},{"value":"Enquiry","label":"core next step"}],"problem":"A visitor choosing a boat needs to know whether it fits their group and budget before making an enquiry. The Fun Cruises Goa artifact contains the building blocks for that decision: a prominent booking action, a description of the experience, fleet filters, and listings with seating capacity and starting hourly prices.","process":["I reviewed the original long-page design as a visitor journey from initial interest to a suitable fleet option. The available PDF shows the landing and fleet-discovery surface. It does not include completed reservation, payment, or availability states, so I describe the scope as discovery and enquiry.","The rates, capacities, address, and response-time copy visible in the old concept are historical design content. This case study uses their placement to explain hierarchy, rather than presenting them as current offers."],"decisions":[{"title":"Bring decision criteria into the fleet list","choice":"The design offers seating, price-order, and boat-type controls, with capacity and starting rate on individual listings.","reason":"A visitor should be able to rule out unsuitable options without opening every boat’s detail page.","tradeoff":"Filters depend on consistent inventory data. Empty results and combinations that return no suitable boat would need explicit handling."},{"title":"Support both ready-to-book and exploratory visitors","choice":"The concept has a hero booking action and an enquiry route alongside fleet details.","reason":"Some visitors know what they want; others need clarification before committing. The page makes both next steps available.","tradeoff":"Booking, getting a quote, and learning more can become competing calls to action. I would clarify which action each listing should lead to."},{"title":"Use imagery with practical content","choice":"Fleet photographs sit beside capacity, starting price, and short descriptions.","reason":"The images convey the experience while the text helps establish whether the option is suitable.","tradeoff":"A starting hourly price does not communicate the full cost. Minimum duration, inclusions, fees, and availability would need to be explained next."}],"outcome":"I produced a website redesign concept connecting a booking invitation to fleet discovery and enquiry. Capacity, starting rate, and boat type give visitors concrete comparison points alongside the imagery. The delivered scope ends at the landing and fleet-browsing surface.","reflection":"The strongest part is the move from generic travel imagery to comparable fleet information. I would now make the boundary between enquiry and confirmed booking unmistakable, so the interface never promises inventory or pricing it cannot verify.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a visitor planning for a group to choose a suitable option, explain the expected cost, and identify what remains to confirm.","Proposed extension: add a boat-detail page with availability, inclusions, accessibility information, cancellation terms, and a clear enquiry handoff."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Hero, quote action, seating/price/type filters, fleet cards and contact footer."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_01.webp","caption":"The upper page connects the experience to a visible booking action and fleet filters.","alt":"Original Fun Cruises Goa hero, introduction and fleet filters."},{"src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_02.webp","caption":"Fleet listings surface practical information next to the experience imagery.","alt":"Original Fun Cruises boat listings with capacity and price content."}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/fun-cruises.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/funcruises.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":21,"narrative":{"headline":"A beautiful boat. [[Does it fit the group?]]","scene":"Picture planning an outing with a group. Before an enquiry, the practical questions arrive: how many people fit, what does it cost, and which type of boat makes sense?","question":"Which questions can the fleet list answer before a visitor opens every option?","insight":"The strongest part of the concept is its [[comparable fleet information]]: seating, starting rate, and boat type accompany the photographs.","pivot":"I treated the landing page as a route into a suitable enquiry, with discovery criteria that narrow the choices.","takeaway":"Good discovery makes the next conversation more informed.","journey":[{"title":"Narrow","detail":"Use capacity, price, and type to find suitable options."},{"title":"Compare","detail":"Read practical information alongside the experience."},{"title":"Enquire","detail":"Confirm availability, inclusions, and the complete cost."}],"decisionLeads":["Answer the comparison questions early.","Support different levels of booking readiness.","Explain what the starting rate leaves to confirm."],"annotations":[{"title":"Decision criteria near the fleet","detail":"Filters appear before the visitor scans individual boats."},{"title":"Imagery with practical content","detail":"Listings pair the experience with capacity and price information."}],"evidenceTag":"Original fleet-discovery concept","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Group organiser considering a boat","context":"Comparing options for a group occasion in Goa.","need":"Identify a suitable boat before making an enquiry.","friction":"Capacity, starting price, and suitability must be read together.","implication":"Keep the practical comparison close to each listing."}},"theme":{"layout":"voyage","mode":"light","paper":"#fffcf6","ink":"#203c40","dim":"#5a7174","line":"#d6ded7","accent":"#866117","wash":"#faedcb","bodyFont":"Lato","displayFont":"Poppins","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Planning for a group","process":"The practical shortlist","decisions":"Designing the enquiry","outcome":"The fleet experience","reflection":"Questions before booking"},"decisionHeading":"Help a group compare the boats.","resultHeading":"A clearer route to a suitable enquiry."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/fun-cruises/p01_02.webp","alt":"Original Fun Cruises boat listings with capacity and price content.","caption":"Fleet listings surface practical information next to the experience imagery.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/fun-cruises.pdf","width":1400,"height":2166},"nextStory":{"slug":"bni-website","name":"BNI Goa","headline":"Make a local business network feel approachable."}},"bni-website":{"slug":"bni-website","name":"BNI Goa","kind":"ux","headline":"Make a local business network feel approachable.","summary":"A chapter-website concept that connects the organisation’s purpose, the people behind it, and a first visit.","role":"Information hierarchy & website visual design","status":"Chapter website concept","context":"BNI Victory Goa · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"People-led","label":"credibility"},{"value":"Visit","label":"primary invitation"},{"value":"Concept","label":"sample member content"}],"problem":"A person considering a business network needs to understand its purpose, who participates, and how to take a first step. The BNI Victory Goa design brings those questions into a local chapter website with an invitation, organisation overview, leadership section, member directory, and contact form.","process":["The available PDF is a long-page visual concept. I reviewed the sequence from the hero invitation through purpose and people to contact. The artifact does not contain an interview report, launch evidence, or a membership funnel.","The member cards repeat placeholder identities and informal sample biographies. The overview also contains network-wide statistics. Neither is evidence of chapter growth or my project impact, so those details are not promoted as results here."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give the visitor a concrete first step","choice":"The hero uses a Get Invited action instead of making the organisation overview the only starting point.","reason":"An invitation turns interest in networking into an understandable next action.","tradeoff":"The next screen would need to explain meeting expectations and eligibility; a button label alone does not establish that service."},{"title":"Make the organisation visible through its people","choice":"Leadership and member-card patterns occupy distinct sections.","reason":"The directory can help a visitor understand the kinds of businesses represented and who is responsible for the chapter.","tradeoff":"Repeated mock content weakens credibility. Production would require member permission, verified biographies, and a reliable update process."},{"title":"Keep purpose and contact in the same journey","choice":"The page connects the chapter overview and mission to a form and local contact details.","reason":"A visitor can move from understanding the organisation to asking a practical question without searching elsewhere.","tradeoff":"The long single-page structure would benefit from a tighter summary and explicit links to meeting details."}],"outcome":"I produced a chapter-site concept with a reusable structure for purpose, people, and participation. The invitation gives prospective visitors a next step, while the leadership and member patterns make the network more tangible. The completed work is a visual concept with sample content.","reflection":"Revisiting the design, content readiness is the most important dependency. A convincing membership experience needs accurate local information and member consent before it needs a larger directory or more prominent global statistics.","nextSteps":["Proposed iteration: replace all sample biographies with verified chapter content and explain the visitor-meeting process before the invitation action.","Proposed evaluation: ask a prospective visitor what the chapter does, whether it is relevant to their business, and what will happen after they request an invitation."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Invitation, organisation overview, mission, leader/member layouts and contact; repeated cards are placeholders."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/bni-website/p01_01.webp","caption":"The original chapter introduction connects the invitation to a purpose-led overview.","alt":"Original BNI Victory Goa hero and organisation overview."}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/bni-website.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/bniwebsite.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":24,"narrative":{"headline":"A network feels local [[when you can see its people]].","scene":"Imagine considering a business network for the first time. You want to understand who participates, why they meet, and whether you could take a first look without already knowing someone.","question":"What turns an organisation overview into an approachable first visit?","insight":"The chapter concept gives the visitor [[a purpose, people, and an invitation]]. Those pieces need accurate local content to become credible.","pivot":"I connected the page structure to a prospective member’s sequence of questions, keeping the invitation visible alongside the chapter story.","takeaway":"Community credibility comes from real people and a clear next step.","journey":[{"title":"Understand","detail":"Learn what the chapter is for."},{"title":"Recognise","detail":"Meet the people and kinds of businesses represented."},{"title":"Visit","detail":"Find out how an invitation works."}],"decisionLeads":["Make a first visit imaginable.","Give responsibility a human face.","Let useful local information carry the invitation."],"annotations":[{"title":"A concrete invitation","detail":"The hero offers a first step into the organisation."},{"title":"A purpose-led introduction","detail":"The overview and mission explain why the chapter exists."}],"evidenceTag":"Original chapter-site concept","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Business owner exploring a local chapter","context":"Considering whether the network is relevant and how to participate.","need":"Understand local people, purpose, and the next step.","friction":"Global organisation information does not answer local membership questions.","implication":"Bring the chapter’s people and participation path forward."}},"theme":{"layout":"chapter","mode":"light","paper":"#fffefe","ink":"#32262a","dim":"#76636a","line":"#ead6dc","accent":"#b7213c","wash":"#fae5e9","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A local membership question","process":"What a chapter must explain","decisions":"Designing participation","outcome":"The chapter concept","reflection":"Content before scale"},"decisionHeading":"Connect the chapter’s purpose to joining.","resultHeading":"A membership story with a next step."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/bni-website/p01_01.webp","alt":"Original BNI Victory Goa hero and organisation overview.","caption":"The original chapter introduction connects the invitation to a purpose-led overview.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/bni-website.pdf","width":1400,"height":2411},"nextStory":{"slug":"irctc","name":"IRCTC","headline":"Put the train search back at the centre."}},"irctc":{"slug":"irctc","name":"IRCTC","kind":"ux","headline":"Put the train search back at the centre.","summary":"An independent homepage concept exploring the hierarchy between a primary booking task and a large set of secondary services.","role":"Task hierarchy & interface redesign concept","status":"Independent redesign concept","context":"Travel booking · No organisational affiliation","facts":[{"value":"Search-first","label":"page hierarchy"},{"value":"5","label":"core journey inputs"},{"value":"Concept","label":"homepage scope"}],"problem":"A railway homepage serves many audiences, but searching for a train is a clear primary task. My independent IRCTC concept places the booking form prominently while retaining entry points for PNR status, vacancies, concessions, and related travel services. The challenge is hierarchy: helping the common task stay visible without erasing important exceptions.","process":["The source is one full homepage design, not a documented end-to-end booking redesign. I evaluated the visible grouping of From, To, Date, Class, and Quota, the surrounding service links, and the separation of holiday content below the search area.","There is no original usability study or live implementation in the provided material. Dates, phone numbers, and offers in the mockup are sample or historical interface content, not current travel guidance."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the core journey fields together","choice":"The concept groups origin, destination, travel date, class, and quota in a single booking panel with a visible Search action.","reason":"The person can form a complete query in one recognisable task area.","tradeoff":"The retained panel still contains many specialised options. Today I would test how to reveal less common settings without hiding essential access needs."},{"title":"Distinguish a current journey from trip inspiration","choice":"Related services and curated holidays appear beneath the primary search region.","reason":"A person trying to book a known route should not have to read promotional content first.","tradeoff":"The holiday cards remain text-heavy. Visual separation helps hierarchy but does not by itself solve page density."},{"title":"Retain explicit entry points for adjacent rail tasks","choice":"PNR status and chart/vacancy shortcuts remain accessible near the booking area.","reason":"People returning to manage an existing journey have a different task from people searching for a new one.","tradeoff":"The homepage only establishes entry points. It does not demonstrate those destination flows or their error handling."}],"outcome":"I produced an independent homepage concept that brings train search into a clear task area and groups secondary services around it. It demonstrates prioritisation within a dense public-service interface. The scope is the homepage concept, with results and booking states left for further development.","reflection":"A clearer first screen is only one part of booking. I would now carry the design into results, availability changes, fare explanation, and recovery from an unavailable route. Those states determine whether the apparent simplicity survives real use.","nextSteps":["Proposed test: find a train, check an existing journey, and locate a concession option; compare discovery and mistakes without assuming that a shorter visual scan means a successful booking.","Proposed extension: add no-results, changing availability, route correction, keyboard navigation, and narrow-screen form states."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Independent homepage mockup showing journey fields, rail-task shortcuts, related services and holidays."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/irctc/p01_01.webp","caption":"The concept makes the booking form the visual centre while retaining adjacent rail tasks.","alt":"Original independent IRCTC redesign with booking panel and service shortcuts."}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/irctc.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/irctc.webp","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":27,"narrative":{"headline":"One journey to book. [[Many things competing for attention]].","scene":"Picture arriving at a railway homepage with a route and a date in mind. Other services matter, but the immediate task is to form a complete train search.","question":"How should a dense service homepage protect its primary task?","insight":"The concept gives the search [[one recognisable task area]], while retaining adjacent rail services and placing trip inspiration further down.","pivot":"I focused the redesign on hierarchy: group the journey inputs, preserve useful shortcuts, and make the difference between booking and browsing legible.","takeaway":"Hierarchy should make the common task clear without erasing important exceptions.","journey":[{"title":"Form the query","detail":"Keep route, date, class, and quota together."},{"title":"Search","detail":"Give the completed query one clear action."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Carry the task into results and recovery states next."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep the primary inputs together.","Let inspiration follow the immediate task.","Preserve a route for people managing an existing journey."],"annotations":[{"title":"A central task area","detail":"The booking panel groups the core journey inputs."},{"title":"Adjacent rail tasks","detail":"PNR and vacancy entry points remain nearby."}],"evidenceTag":"Independent homepage concept","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Traveller starting a rail search","context":"Arriving with a route and a possible date in mind.","need":"Start a train search without losing the primary task.","friction":"Many services and promotions compete for attention.","implication":"Give journey inputs first priority."}},"theme":{"layout":"departure","mode":"light","paper":"#f8faff","ink":"#162c50","dim":"#536785","line":"#d0dced","accent":"#253a96","wash":"#e5eafa","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A journey to begin","process":"The task worth protecting","decisions":"Clearing the search path","outcome":"The homepage concept","reflection":"Testing the first search"},"decisionHeading":"Keep train booking at the centre.","resultHeading":"Search leads. Other services follow."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/cases/irctc/p01_01.webp","alt":"Original independent IRCTC redesign with booking panel and service shortcuts.","caption":"The concept makes the booking form the visual centre while retaining adjacent rail tasks.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/irctc.pdf","width":1400,"height":1969},"nextStory":{"slug":"nityananda","name":"Nitya Nanda","headline":"Let the photographs lead, then make contact easy."}},"nityananda":{"slug":"nityananda","name":"Nitya Nanda","kind":"ux","headline":"Let the photographs lead, then make contact easy.","summary":"A photography portfolio concept with a deliberately restrained interface and two clear ways into the work.","role":"Portfolio structure & visual design","status":"Independent portfolio concept","context":"Photography · Archive retrospective","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"work categories"},{"value":"3","label":"primary destinations"},{"value":"Image-led","label":"visual hierarchy"}],"problem":"A photography portfolio needs to show the work before asking visitors to read a long introduction. The Nitya Nanda concept explores a restrained structure with About, Work, and Contact destinations, an image-led opening, and separate professional and freelance collections.","process":["The available source is a single long visual concept. I traced how the page introduces the photographer, groups the work, and leads to the contact form. The listed shoots, locations, and credentials are illustrative portfolio content; the artifact does not substantiate them as real commissions.","This retrospective draws design observations from the page itself. No original audience research, client engagement record, or booking measurements accompany the concept."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep the navigation vocabulary small","choice":"The page repeats About, Work, and Contact around an image-led presentation.","reason":"These are familiar questions for someone considering a creative professional and make the portfolio easy to orient within.","tradeoff":"Repeated navigation should help orientation rather than consume space. Today I would test a single persistent version on smaller screens."},{"title":"Separate two ways of reading the portfolio","choice":"Professional and freelance work have their own grouped image collections with captions.","reason":"The distinction gives visitors a route based on the kind of commission they are considering.","tradeoff":"The grouping is a design assumption. Real enquiries might be better served by subject, service, or photographic style instead."},{"title":"Give the visual journey a practical endpoint","choice":"A short contact form follows the portfolio, with additional work and consultation links.","reason":"Someone convinced by the images should have a direct route to starting a conversation.","tradeoff":"The concept stops at submission. A complete service needs clear expectations, error messages, and confirmation of what happens next."}],"outcome":"I produced an image-led portfolio concept with professional and freelance collections, a restrained navigation vocabulary, and a contact destination. The layout demonstrates how an interface can support a creative body of work without becoming its main subject. The photography credentials in the artifact are illustrative content.","reflection":"The main improvement I would make is editorial: fewer unsubstantiated biographical claims, stronger captions, and one clear enquiry path. For a photography site, useful image descriptions and fast loading would be part of the design brief from the start.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask visitors choosing a photographer to identify a relevant body of work and start an enquiry; observe which category language they expect.","Proposed implementation: responsive image sizes, descriptive alternatives, keyboard access, and a complete form confirmation and error flow."],"sources":[{"label":"Original artifact, p. 1","detail":"Photographer introduction, professional/freelance galleries, contact form and illustrative project captions."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/cases/nityananda/p01.webp","caption":"The original full-page concept: images carry the story and contact closes the sequence.","alt":"Original Nitya Nanda photography portfolio layout."}],"featured":false,"originalPdf":"assets/pdfs/nityananda.pdf","prototypeUrl":null,"cover":"assets/work/nityananda.webp","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":30,"narrative":{"headline":"Let the work speak. [[Make the conversation easy]].","scene":"Imagine choosing a photographer. You want to see relevant work, get a feel for the person behind it, and know how to begin an enquiry.","question":"How much interface does an image-led portfolio actually need?","insight":"The concept uses [[three familiar destinations]] and two work groupings to keep the route through the photography understandable.","pivot":"I treated restraint as a structural choice: let the images carry the first impression, then give the visitor a practical endpoint.","takeaway":"A quiet interface still needs to give clear directions.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Let images establish the first impression."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Choose a relevant collection."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Start an enquiry with clear expectations."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep orientation familiar.","Group the work around a useful distinction.","Give the visual journey a practical endpoint."],"annotations":[{"title":"Images carry the story","detail":"The concept keeps the interface around a large body of work."},{"title":"Contact closes the sequence","detail":"The enquiry form gives interest somewhere to go."}],"evidenceTag":"Original photography-portfolio concept","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person considering a photographer","context":"Looking for a relevant style and a way to discuss a shoot.","need":"See a coherent body of work and begin an enquiry.","friction":"A broad gallery can make relevance hard to judge.","implication":"Use collections and a clear contact route."}},"theme":{"layout":"gallery","mode":"dark","paper":"#101010","ink":"#f2eee8","dim":"#bcb5ac","line":"#3d3832","accent":"#d9c6a9","wash":"#342e26","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Cormorant Garamond","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Looking for a style","process":"Letting the work speak","decisions":"A portfolio with space","outcome":"The photography concept","reflection":"Making relevance clearer"},"decisionHeading":"Let visitors browse the photographs.","resultHeading":"A body of work before a sales pitch."},"visual":{"presentation":"split","src":"assets/cases/nityananda/p01.webp","alt":"Original Nitya Nanda photography portfolio layout.","caption":"The original full-page concept: images carry the story and contact closes the sequence.","label":"Original design artifact","source":"assets/pdfs/nityananda.pdf","width":1400,"height":3557},"nextStory":{"slug":"arkitype","name":"Arkitype","headline":"Make a design system a set of connected decisions."}},"arkitype":{"slug":"arkitype","name":"Arkitype","kind":"product","headline":"Make a design system a set of connected decisions.","summary":"A working design-system studio that links foundations, component behaviour, previews, and exports through one model.","role":"Product idea, design-system UX & interaction direction","status":"Public beta","context":"Design systems · Working product","facts":[{"value":"90","label":"components in the system registry"},{"value":"8","label":"decision areas"},{"value":"Shared","label":"token-to-export model"}],"problem":"A palette, a component library, and a handoff document can each look convincing while disagreeing with one another. I approached Arkitype as a connected workspace: decisions about colour, type, space, shape, and motion should remain visible in the components and artifacts they produce.","process":["I framed the workspace around eight connected design areas, rather than a compulsory wizard. A designer should be able to revisit a foundation while considering a component and still understand what depends on that decision.","The UX goal is continuity: foundations, component states, previews, and handoff should feel like parts of one system. The current 90-component library is product scope; it does not establish that a new user understands the workspace."],"decisions":[{"title":"Use dependencies to guide, rather than enforce a sequence","choice":"Foundations, assets, components, and preview remain accessible, with relationships expressed as Built from and Lands in links.","reason":"The navigation reflects iterative design work instead of pretending every system starts and ends in the same order.","tradeoff":"An open workspace gives less automatic direction. First-use guidance must help a person find a useful starting point."},{"title":"Keep design changes consistent across surfaces","choice":"The preview, component workspace, and handoff reflect the same design decisions.","reason":"A change should carry through to the places a team actually uses, rather than depend on repeated manual updates.","tradeoff":"A designer needs to know which properties carry through and which need review before handoff."},{"title":"Let people inspect appearance and behaviour","choice":"The studio distinguishes controlled component specimens from interactive previews.","reason":"A design system needs comparable states and realistic behaviour. One view cannot do both equally well.","tradeoff":"Interactive examples add state and complexity; the underlying specimen must remain predictable for documentation and export."}],"outcome":"The resulting beta connects editable foundations, a component studio, previews, contrast checks, and handoff. My case-study focus is the design continuity between those surfaces. The 90-component scope makes that direction tangible; first-use clarity remains to be evaluated.","reflection":"The central lesson is that system quality comes from relationships, not inventory. I would evaluate whether a new designer can make a coherent change, understand where it lands, and hand it off without a second interpretation step.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a designer to start from one brand colour, adjust a component, and produce a usable handoff. Record confusion, corrections, and output mismatches.","Proposed measure: time to the first coherent system and the number of inconsistencies discovered after export, with a defined task and baseline."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and lib/systemAreas.ts","detail":"Eight-area navigation, dependency graph, specimen/interactive distinction, beta scope and export pipeline."},{"label":"lib/componentLanes.ts and docs/GETTING_STARTED.md","detail":"Component registry and the documented first-use journey. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://arkitype.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/arkitype.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":3,"narrative":{"headline":"Change one token. [[Watch the system follow]].","scene":"Imagine changing a brand colour and then checking every place it appears: components, documentation, previews, and the handoff. The change is small; keeping all its consequences aligned is the real work.","question":"What if the different outputs all came from the same decision?","insight":"A system needs [[relationships between decisions]], not just an inventory of components. Arkitype’s shared model connects foundations to the things a team can inspect and use.","pivot":"I organised the workspace around dependencies, letting designers revisit decisions while keeping their consequences visible.","takeaway":"System quality lives in what stays connected when something changes.","journey":[{"title":"Define","detail":"Set colour, type, space, shape, and motion."},{"title":"Bind","detail":"Connect component behaviour to system tokens."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"See the decisions in a real preview."},{"title":"Ship","detail":"Generate artifacts from the same model."}],"decisionLeads":["Let the work move in more than one direction.","Make one change travel through the system.","Show both the specimen and the behaviour."],"annotations":[{"title":"A working system","detail":"The archived capture introduces the actual product, rather than a speculative redesign."},{"title":"Follow a change","detail":"The palette and contrast inspector keep a foundation choice beside the information needed to evaluate it."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · shared model","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Designer shaping a shared system","context":"Revisiting foundations and component decisions during an evolving project.","need":"Understand how one decision affects the rest of the system.","friction":"Disconnected screens and handoff artifacts can drift.","implication":"Show relationships, states, and consequences together."}},"theme":{"layout":"system","mode":"light","paper":"#e8e8eb","ink":"#24242a","dim":"#5c5c68","line":"#bcbcc6","accent":"#4e3cad","wash":"#e1def2","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Space Grotesk","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A system in motion","process":"Relationships over inventory","decisions":"The workspace contract","outcome":"From decisions to handoff","reflection":"The first-use question"},"decisionHeading":"Keep design changes connected.","resultHeading":"One system, visible in several places."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/arkitype-workspace.jpg","alt":"Arkitype workspace showing foundation palettes, navigation and contrast inspector.","caption":"Foundations in context: the palette, navigation and contrast inspector share one workspace.","label":"Product interface","source":"Arkitype/docs/images/workspace.jpg","focus":[{"title":"Navigate the system","detail":"Foundations and components remain within reach."},{"title":"See the consequence","detail":"Editable palettes stay beside the result."},{"title":"Inspect the decision","detail":"Contrast information sits in the same workspace."}],"width":1280,"height":720,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":0,"y":60,"width":550,"height":360,"caption":"Foundations and components stay available in the workspace navigation.","alt":"Detail of the original Arkitype navigation beside the foundation palette."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"applecider","name":"AppleCider","headline":"Keep early ideas easy to change."}},"applecider":{"slug":"applecider","name":"AppleCider","kind":"product","headline":"Keep early ideas easy to change.","summary":"A sketch-like wireframing workspace that carries rough screens into clickable prototypes and editable handoff artifacts.","role":"Product idea, sketching workflow & interaction design","status":"Working product","context":"Design tools · Working product","facts":[{"value":"250","label":"documented library components"},{"value":"3","label":"sketch styles"},{"value":"Local","label":"browser-owned files"}],"problem":"Early design conversations can become arguments about polish before the underlying idea is settled. I framed AppleCider around an intentionally unfinished visual language: the speed and social permission of a sketch, with the editing capabilities of a digital workspace.","process":["I separated the experience into a welcoming introduction, a files workspace, and the editing canvas. This gives each moment a clear task: understand the tool, find the work, then explore an idea.","The design loop is sketch, revise, demonstrate, and hand off. Components, undo, snapping, links, and export matter because they let someone change their mind without starting again. Formal user validation remains a next step."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the unfinished quality deliberate","choice":"Wobbly strokes, a sketch-like type treatment, and three visual styles preserve a low-fidelity feel.","reason":"The interface should invite changes to the idea before encouraging arguments about pixel-level finish.","tradeoff":"A sketch aesthetic can also reduce legibility. Labels, focus states, and interactions still need the clarity of a serious tool."},{"title":"Keep the work portable","choice":"The files surface stores documents locally and supports a plain project-file export, alongside image and vector outputs.","reason":"A person should be able to keep and move their work without an account or a proprietary cloud workflow.","tradeoff":"Browser storage is device-specific and can be cleared. File ownership needs an understandable backup and export path."},{"title":"Carry structure into the next artifact","choice":"The handoff offers editable output and a written specification, alongside the clickable prototype.","reason":"The transition from a rough screen to a working conversation should preserve the decisions already made.","tradeoff":"Generated structure is a starting point. Placeholder copy and sketch styling should not be presented as finished product requirements."}],"outcome":"The resulting product connects a sketch canvas, files workspace, clickable prototypes, and handoff formats. The documented 250-component scope supports exploration, while the deliberately unfinished visual language leaves room for revision. My contribution centres on that idea and experience direction.","reflection":"The most valuable bridge is between fidelity and commitment. A rough interface can still have meaningful structure, and a useful handoff should preserve that structure without inventing design decisions the sketch never made.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: give participants an early product idea, ask them to revise it after feedback, and then explain the exported prototype to someone else.","Proposed measure: time to the first understandable screen, effort to revise its structure, and corrections needed after export."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and docs/architecture.md","detail":"Visual intent, component library, three surfaces, local file model and storage trade-off."},{"label":"src/io/export.ts and src/store/store.ts","detail":"Export and document-state behaviour supporting the documented workflow. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://applecider.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/applecider.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":5,"pdfPage":6,"narrative":{"headline":"Keep the idea rough. [[Make it real enough to discuss]].","scene":"Picture an early design review drifting into a discussion about colour and spacing. The underlying idea is still changing, but the polished screen already feels too finished to challenge.","question":"Can a deliberately unfinished interface still have useful structure?","insight":"[[Low visual fidelity can preserve room to change]]. Clickable connections and portable outputs let a rough idea move into the next design conversation.","pivot":"I kept the sketch-like visual language and gave it the capabilities of a working design tool: move, revise, link, and hand off.","takeaway":"Commit to the structure only as fast as the idea earns it.","journey":[{"title":"Sketch","detail":"Get an idea onto a movable canvas."},{"title":"Revise","detail":"Change the structure while it still feels disposable."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Link the screens into a prototype."},{"title":"Carry forward","detail":"Export the structure for the next conversation."}],"decisionLeads":["Make unfinished feel intentional.","Give the person ownership of the work.","Carry the structure into the next artifact."],"annotations":[{"title":"The sketch is the point","detail":"The visual language makes the stage of the work clear."},{"title":"Structure underneath","detail":"Editable components and exports give the rough screen a useful next step."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · sketch and handoff workflow","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Designer discussing an early idea","context":"Exploring structure before investing in final visual polish.","need":"Make an idea concrete while keeping it easy to change.","friction":"A finished-looking mockup can make tentative decisions feel settled.","implication":"Keep the drawing rough and the interactions usable."}},"theme":{"layout":"sketchbook","mode":"light","paper":"#fffdf8","ink":"#2f2b25","dim":"#6c6256","line":"#d7cdbd","accent":"#a33a20","wash":"#f8e5d7","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"An idea still in pencil","process":"Keep it changeable","decisions":"Rough on purpose","outcome":"A sketch you can discuss","reflection":"What I would try with designers"},"decisionHeading":"Keep early ideas easy to revise.","resultHeading":"Rough enough to revise. Real enough to discuss."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/applecider.webp","alt":"AppleCider — archived product interface","caption":"The archived homepage demonstrates the intentionally rough visual language. These are the project’s own wireframe examples.","label":"Original wireframe examples","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"planit","name":"PlanIt","headline":"Plan for the group without flattening everyone into one schedule."}},"planit":{"slug":"planit","name":"PlanIt","kind":"product","headline":"Plan for the group without flattening everyone into one schedule.","summary":"A browser-based itinerary workspace for overlapping plans, different travellers, and different time zones.","role":"Product framing, group-planning journeys & interaction design","status":"Working product","context":"Group planning · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Shared","label":"plan across multiple views"},{"value":"Explicit","label":"board-to-time action"},{"value":"Local","label":"no account required"}],"problem":"A group trip is not a single ordered checklist. Different people arrive at different times, attend parallel activities, and move between places. I approached PlanIt as several useful views of one plan, with a board for thinking and a time model for checking whether that plan can work.","process":["I distinguished tentative ideas from scheduled commitments. The canvas supports exploration, while itinerary, timeline, map, and people views help the group understand the consequences of a plan.","The key interaction is the deliberate move from arrangement to schedule. Pins protect an existing commitment; loose ideas can stay undecided. The UX needs to explain what Resolve changes and make conflicts understandable."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give the board a clear but limited grammar","choice":"Day frames, ordering, and connectors convey meaning; loose cards can remain uncommitted ideas.","reason":"A planning surface needs room for uncertainty instead of assigning a date and time to everything immediately.","tradeoff":"Spatial meaning must be taught. A frame or connector that looks decorative can otherwise change a plan unexpectedly."},{"title":"Make scheduling an intentional action","choice":"Tidy turns the existing itinerary into a board; Resolve reads the board back into the schedule.","reason":"A person should know when arrangement becomes a commitment. Automatic rearrangement would undermine the value of a free board.","tradeoff":"Two explicit directions add a concept to learn, so the product needs visible feedback about what changed and why."},{"title":"Protect decisions the planner has already made","choice":"Pinned times win, untouched loose cards opt out, and resolution only moves times forward when constraints require it.","reason":"Automation should respect existing intent and explain an impossible plan rather than silently rewrite it.","tradeoff":"A conservative resolver may leave conflicts unresolved. The interface must help the person decide what to relax."}],"outcome":"The resulting product brings spatial planning, schedules, maps, and people into a shared trip. Its defining UX contract is deliberate scheduling, protected commitments, and visible conflicts. The next question is whether planners understand that contract when they first encounter it.","reflection":"The main design contribution is the contract around automation. The useful question is not simply whether the app can assign a time, but whether a planner can predict the change and recover when it conflicts with a real commitment.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: plan a trip with parallel sessions, a pinned departure, and travellers in two zones; ask participants to predict Resolve before using it.","Proposed measure: unexpected schedule changes, correctly identified conflicts, and confidence explaining the final plan."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Planning views, board grammar, sharing model and scheduling constraints."},{"label":"src/core/resolve.ts and src/core/store.ts","detail":"Priority of pins, opt-in cards and forward-only resolution. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://planit.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/planit.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":9,"narrative":{"headline":"The group is travelling together. [[Their plans are not identical]].","scene":"Imagine one person at a conference session, another arriving later, and a shared dinner across town. Put it all into one simple list and the useful differences disappear.","question":"When should a loose idea become a scheduled commitment?","insight":"A board needs room for uncertainty. [[Scheduling should be an explicit decision]], with existing commitments protected.","pivot":"I separated thinking on the board from committing to the schedule, with clear UX rules for what automation is allowed to change.","takeaway":"Useful automation makes its changes predictable.","journey":[{"title":"Arrange","detail":"Use frames and connectors to express intent."},{"title":"Protect","detail":"Keep pinned times as commitments."},{"title":"Resolve","detail":"Translate the arrangement into a schedule deliberately."},{"title":"Explain","detail":"Make remaining conflicts visible to the planner."}],"decisionLeads":["Let an idea stay an idea.","Make the moment of commitment visible.","Give automation a boundary it cannot cross."],"annotations":[{"title":"Several views, one plan","detail":"The working product carries the same trip across planning surfaces."},{"title":"An explicit contract","detail":"Pins, opt-in cards, and forward-only changes define the resolver."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · scheduling rules","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person coordinating a group trip","context":"Combining people with different arrivals, activities, and commitments.","need":"Turn possibilities into a plan while protecting confirmed times.","friction":"A single list hides overlaps and treats ideas as promises.","implication":"Separate tentative cards from explicit scheduling."}},"theme":{"layout":"planner","mode":"dark","paper":"#0d0f17","ink":"#edeff7","dim":"#a8b0c6","line":"#343e56","accent":"#ffb094","wash":"#35251f","bodyFont":"Instrument Sans","displayFont":"Instrument Sans","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"People with different plans","process":"Ideas are not commitments","decisions":"The scheduling contract","outcome":"The shared plan","reflection":"Where understanding can break"},"decisionHeading":"Separate ideas from confirmed plans.","resultHeading":"One plan, with room for different people."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/planit.webp","alt":"PlanIt — archived product interface","caption":"The archived PlanIt interface. Ideas, scheduled commitments and location are treated as different views of a shared trip.","label":"Planning workspace","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"nextStory":{"slug":"earthlog","name":"Earthlog","headline":"Give a place a story, without hiding the uncertainty."}},"earthlog":{"slug":"earthlog","name":"Earthlog","kind":"product","headline":"Give a place a story, without hiding the uncertainty.","summary":"Explore earthquakes, weather, and the night sky from one place on a globe.","role":"Experience framing, information hierarchy & map interactions","status":"Working product","context":"Data exploration · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"directions of exploration"},{"value":"Place-led","label":"event context"},{"value":"Labelled","label":"models and estimates"}],"problem":"A place can have recent earthquakes, old incident records, current weather, and predicted sky events. Bringing them onto one map makes exploration easier, but can also make very different information look equally current and reliable.","process":["I framed two ways into exploration: start from a place, or follow an interesting event to its location. Both routes should arrive at a readable local picture, rather than another layer to decode.","Freshness and uncertainty are part of the information hierarchy. An observation, an older open record, and a forecast need different labels so the map does not make them look equally current or certain."],"decisions":[{"title":"Start with a place or a question","choice":"People can search for a place, select the globe, or follow an event shortcut.","reason":"Someone may be curious about the latest earthquake without knowing where it happened.","tradeoff":"Every route needs to lead back to the same place context."},{"title":"Show when the information was observed","choice":"The documented event model uses observation recency instead of treating every open incident as current.","reason":"A source’s administrative status can outlive the actual event. The label should communicate what is known.","tradeoff":"Freshness rules remain approximations; source links and timestamps must stay visible."},{"title":"Explain the limits beside the result","choice":"Eclipse paths, station passes, and aurora layers carry model limitations in the documented product.","reason":"A compelling map should not appear more precise than the calculation behind it.","tradeoff":"Caveats can overwhelm the visual story, so they belong beside the relevant result rather than only in documentation."}],"outcome":"The working globe supports both place search and event discovery. Dates, source links, and model limitations are part of the product direction. I would still need to test whether visitors understand those distinctions.","reflection":"I would judge this experience by whether someone can explain what a result means and where it came from, as well as whether they can find it.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: ask visitors to distinguish a recent observation from a modelled forecast and locate the source behind each result.","Proposed measure: correct interpretation of freshness and uncertainty, alongside success finding a relevant local event."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What it answers and Honesty notes","detail":"Bidirectional exploration, source freshness and model limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://earthlog.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/earthlog.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":12,"narrative":{"headline":"Pick a place. [[What has Earth been doing here?]]","scene":"Imagine looking at a place on a globe and wanting more than a pin: the latest quake, tonight’s sky, or the weather around it. Those answers arrive from different sources with different levels of certainty.","question":"What is happening here—and how current is the information?","insight":"An incident can remain open after the last observation. [[A status label is not enough]] to explain whether something is happening now.","pivot":"I kept dates, sources, and model limits alongside the result, so visitors can judge the information while exploring.","takeaway":"Make the answer easy to find and easy to question.","journey":[{"title":"Choose","detail":"Start with a place or an interesting event."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Read the local context across sources."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"See when the information was observed and how it was derived."}],"decisionLeads":["Offer a question as well as a place.","Make freshness more than a status label.","Keep uncertainty beside the answer."],"annotations":[{"title":"A place-led dossier","detail":"The capture shows how the globe connects to local information."},{"title":"An explanation with boundaries","detail":"Model limits remain part of the result."}],"evidenceTag":"Based on the documented freshness rules and source labels.","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"People exploring a place or following an event","context":"Following an event or asking what is happening near a location.","need":"Find local context and understand how current it is.","friction":"Different observations and forecasts can appear equally certain on a map.","implication":"Keep timestamps, sources, and uncertainty beside the result."}},"theme":{"layout":"atlas","mode":"dark","paper":"#06080b","ink":"#ece9e3","dim":"#b6bac1","line":"#454b55","accent":"#f2a93b","wash":"#33271a","bodyFont":"Public Sans","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Start with a place","process":"Keep the date beside the answer","decisions":"Reading an uncertain world","outcome":"The local picture","reflection":"What the map implies"},"decisionHeading":"Three choices behind the exploration","resultHeading":"One place to explore several kinds of information"},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/earthlog.webp","alt":"Earthlog — archived product interface","caption":"The actual exploration surface: globe and event entry points on the left; place, weather, sky and history in the inspector.","label":"Globe and place inspector","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","focus":[{"title":"Enter through a place","detail":"Search and globe selection start from a location."},{"title":"Enter through an event","detail":"Event shortcuts reverse the route into the same place context."},{"title":"Read in context","detail":"The inspector separates place, weather, sky and history."}],"width":1280,"height":720,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":14,"y":488,"width":500,"height":192,"caption":"Event shortcuts let a visitor start with a question before choosing a place.","alt":"Original Earthlog event shortcuts for earthquakes, eclipses, the space station and other events."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"hexchess","name":"HexChess","headline":"Teach the unfamiliar board through play."}},"hexchess":{"slug":"hexchess","name":"HexChess","kind":"product","headline":"Teach the unfamiliar board through play.","summary":"A 91-cell chess variant designed around guided learning, readable interaction, and games that survive interruptions.","role":"Learning journey, onboarding & game interactions","status":"Working product","context":"Learning & play · Working product","facts":[{"value":"91","label":"board cells"},{"value":"6","label":"guided piece lessons"},{"value":"Offline","label":"after first successful load"}],"problem":"A hexagonal board changes movement patterns enough to make familiar chess knowledge unreliable. I designed HexChess around learning by trying: clear targets, feedback on moves, and a route into untimed practice instead of expecting a beginner to absorb a rules document first.","process":["I connected a six-part introduction to the first practice game. A learner should encounter a rule on the board, try a move, and carry that understanding into play.","I treated interruption and sharing as parts of the experience. A saved game needs a clear return path, and a correspondence link needs to explain that the other person replies with an updated game rather than joins a live room."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make movement teachable on the board","choice":"Lessons give a specific target, neutral markers, retries, and progress based on actual practice.","reason":"A learner can connect a rule to a move instead of memorising abstract directions.","tradeoff":"Move previews and legal moves serve different purposes; the interface must explain when inspection ignores king safety."},{"title":"Keep assistance appropriate to the context","choice":"Hints and takebacks belong to computer practice; human games remove assistance and pause controls.","reason":"Learning support should not silently change the rules of a friendly competitive game.","tradeoff":"Assistance rules need to be clear to both players; the interface is not evidence of tournament-grade enforcement."},{"title":"Describe sharing accurately","choice":"Correspondence links carry move history and require an updated reply after a turn.","reason":"People should understand whether they are entering a synchronized room or receiving a game snapshot.","tradeoff":"Long histories create long URLs, and a shared snapshot is readable by anyone given the link."}],"outcome":"The resulting game offers a 91-cell board, six guided lessons, practice, saved sessions, and correspondence sharing. The design contribution connects the first explanation to the first usable move; improved learning has not been measured.","reflection":"Good teaching interaction makes a wrong move recoverable. I would evaluate whether someone can transfer what they learned into a first game, rather than treating tutorial completion alone as success.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: observe a new player complete a piece lesson and use the same movement unaided in practice.","Proposed checks: reload during a game, resume offline, and exchange a correspondence reply while confirming both players understand the snapshot model."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Learning, board interactions, saves, offline support, sharing boundaries and existing test scope. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hexchess.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/hexchess-live.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":15,"narrative":{"headline":"You know chess. [[The board has changed]].","scene":"Imagine recognising the pieces but no longer trusting your instinct for where they can move. A rules page can explain the geometry; a first move makes it tangible.","question":"How can the board become part of the teaching?","insight":"[[Practice makes a rule concrete]]. Six guided piece lessons lead into an untimed first game with move highlights.","pivot":"I brought explanation into the interaction: a target, an attempt, feedback, and a recoverable retry.","takeaway":"Teach the next useful action, then give someone room to try it.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Give the lesson one clear target."},{"title":"Try","detail":"Let the learner select and move."},{"title":"Recover","detail":"Explain a wrong attempt without ending the lesson."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Transfer the movement into beginner practice."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the rule visible where it is used.","Keep practice help appropriate to the game.","Describe what a shared game really is."],"annotations":[{"title":"The board stays primary","detail":"The playing room keeps the central interaction prominent."},{"title":"Progress survives interruption","detail":"Local saves and offline support belong to the game experience."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · six guided lessons","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Chess player learning a different board","context":"Recognising familiar pieces in an unfamiliar hexagonal geometry.","need":"Understand a movement rule and try it safely.","friction":"Abstract directions do not automatically translate into a playable move.","implication":"Teach on the board, then open guided practice."}},"theme":{"layout":"lesson","mode":"light","paper":"#f6f6f3","ink":"#32363b","dim":"#62696e","line":"#d2d5d6","accent":"#46584c","wash":"#e1e7db","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Libre Caslon Display","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A familiar piece, a new board","process":"Learning through a move","decisions":"From hint to understanding","outcome":"The first game","reflection":"What practice would reveal"},"decisionHeading":"Teach the board through the next move.","resultHeading":"From the first lesson into play."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/hexchess-live.webp","alt":"HexChess — archived product interface","caption":"The original playing room: a 91-cell board, legal-move guidance and game controls.","label":"Playing interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","focus":[{"title":"Understand the board","detail":"The playing surface carries the movement explanation."},{"title":"Try a move","detail":"Guidance stays in the context of play."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Practice and a saved game support a return to the board."}],"width":1265,"height":949},"nextStory":{"slug":"dashf1","name":"DashF1","headline":"Keep the race understandable when the data is uneven."}},"dashf1":{"slug":"dashf1","name":"DashF1","kind":"product","headline":"Keep the race understandable when the data is uneven.","summary":"A race dashboard connecting timing, track context, and replay while distinguishing measured data from estimated positions.","role":"Dashboard hierarchy, driver focus & information design","status":"Working product","context":"Live data · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"driver focus across panels"},{"value":"Live / replay","label":"distinct data modes"},{"value":"Estimated","label":"free-feed track positions"}],"problem":"A race weekend produces more signals than a person can follow at once, and the available feed changes between live and replay sessions. DashF1 uses one shared driver focus across the timing tower, map, and detail views so the dashboard supports a question rather than a wall of independent charts.","process":["I organised the experience around one driver focus. Timing, track context, and detail panels should answer related questions without requiring the same selection repeatedly.","I separated live and replay expectations. When a position is estimated, the label belongs near the moving marker; missing coverage should be understandable at the moment someone tries to use it."],"decisions":[{"title":"Carry one selection through the dashboard","choice":"Choosing a driver updates the relevant map and telemetry context.","reason":"The person should not have to repeat the same selection in several panels.","tradeoff":"The relationship must remain clear when the selected driver retires or a particular data channel is unavailable."},{"title":"Let the mode determine the promise","choice":"Live mode shows supported channels; fuller replay data becomes available through a separate path.","reason":"Missing data should change the interface’s promise instead of being replaced with plausible-looking numbers.","tradeoff":"A mode change can alter familiar panels, so the dashboard needs an understandable explanation."},{"title":"Label an estimate at the point of use","choice":"The documented free-feed map derives position from mini-sector timing and gaps, and calls it estimated.","reason":"Visual continuity is useful, but it should not be mistaken for location precision the feed does not provide.","tradeoff":"Race interruptions and stale timing can challenge the estimate; smooth motion must not conceal those limits."}],"outcome":"The dashboard brings driver focus, track context, timing, and replay together. The UX direction keeps related panels in context and makes differences in data coverage visible. It does not establish improved race comprehension or precision.","reflection":"The design lesson is that trust depends on making uneven coverage understandable. A useful dashboard can show less information when it knows less, while still helping a person follow the race.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a viewer to follow a battle and explain which values are observed, historical, or estimated.","Proposed checks: missing telemetry, stale updates, interrupted streams, safety-car periods, and the transition back to replay."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What’s on screen and Race mode","detail":"Layout, shared focus, data-source boundaries and position-estimation limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://f1dash.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/f1dash-cover.svg","coverKind":"cover","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":18,"narrative":{"headline":"Follow the battle. [[Know what the feed knows]].","scene":"Imagine following two drivers through a race weekend. Timing, position, tyres, and telemetry compete for attention—and not every channel is available in every session.","question":"What should the dashboard do when its data coverage changes?","insight":"A smooth marker can still be an estimate. [[The mode should determine the promise]] the interface makes.","pivot":"I used one driver focus across the dashboard and separate live/replay modes that respect the available information.","takeaway":"A useful dashboard can say less when it knows less.","journey":[{"title":"Focus","detail":"Choose the driver once."},{"title":"Connect","detail":"Carry that focus across panels."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"Distinguish measured channels from estimated positions."}],"decisionLeads":["Carry one question across the dashboard.","Let the available data set the scope.","Make a moving estimate visibly an estimate."],"annotations":[{"title":"Shared focus","detail":"A selected driver carries across timing, track position and available detail."},{"title":"Coverage has boundaries","detail":"The product distinguishes live estimates from richer replay data."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · live and replay modes","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Race follower tracking a driver","context":"Moving between timing, track position, and available detail.","need":"Keep one driver in context while following the race.","friction":"Repeated selections and uneven data coverage disrupt the story.","implication":"Synchronise focus and explain each mode’s coverage."}},"theme":{"layout":"race","mode":"dark","paper":"#0b0e14","ink":"#eef1f6","dim":"#b3bccb","line":"#394454","accent":"#ff6a61","wash":"#3a1e24","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Space Grotesk","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A driver to follow","process":"One focus across panels","decisions":"The dashboard contract","outcome":"The race in context","reflection":"Knowing what is estimated"},"decisionHeading":"Keep one driver in focus across the dashboard.","resultHeading":"Follow the driver through the available data."},"visual":{"presentation":"type","label":"Product direction","caption":"A race dashboard connecting timing, track context, and replay while distinguishing measured data from estimated positions.","source":"Product scope and project documentation"},"nextStory":{"slug":"hued","name":"Hued","headline":"Turn one colour into a reason to notice the world."}},"hued":{"slug":"hued","name":"Hued","kind":"product","headline":"Turn one colour into a reason to notice the world.","summary":"A daily photography prompt with a shared constraint, on-device scoring, and a warm visual language.","role":"Product concept, creative loop & visual direction","status":"Mobile beta","context":"Creative habits · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"shared daily colour"},{"value":"48","label":"colours in the documented palette"},{"value":"On-device","label":"colour scoring"}],"problem":"A broad prompt to be creative can be harder to act on than a small constraint. Hued gives everyone the same daily colour and asks them to find and photograph it in the real world. The product connects a simple creative action to a repeatable daily rhythm.","process":["I shaped the concept around one shared colour and a small creative invitation: notice it in the world, photograph it, and see other interpretations. The daily boundary needs a clear local explanation.","A quiet, warm interface keeps the photographs in front. Scores and streaks are supporting feedback; whether they encourage curiosity or pressure is a question for evaluation, not an established habit-formation result."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make the prompt shared and specific","choice":"One date-derived colour creates a common constraint for the day.","reason":"A small, concrete invitation can make starting easier and make different responses interesting to compare.","tradeoff":"A UTC boundary may feel surprising across time zones; the deadline needs to be understandable locally."},{"title":"Keep the creative result in the foreground","choice":"The feed emphasises photographs within a quiet, warm visual system.","reason":"The person’s interpretation of the colour should carry more attention than surrounding interface decoration.","tradeoff":"Ranking and streaks can compete with exploration; their value needs to be judged from behaviour and feedback."},{"title":"Treat scoring as guidance with limits","choice":"Colour matching runs locally; suspicious-image heuristics are documented as tentative rather than proof.","reason":"A score can provide feedback without pretending to reliably determine how an image was made.","tradeoff":"Lighting, camera processing, and scene composition affect colour. An unexplained penalty can feel arbitrary."}],"outcome":"The mobile beta makes the daily colour loop tangible through a prompt, photographs, feedback, and a shared feed. My contribution is the creative concept and experience direction. Repeat participation and perceived creative value remain to be evaluated.","reflection":"I would judge this product by whether it encourages satisfying acts of noticing, not just a streak count. The score should help explain the colour challenge without becoming an unquestionable verdict on creativity.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: compare how people interpret the prompt and score under different lighting conditions, including whether they understand a low score.","Proposed measure: repeat participation with self-reported creative value; evaluate deadline understanding across time zones."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/dailyColor.ts","detail":"Daily prompt, palette and beta scope."},{"label":"src/lib/scoring.ts and README limitations","detail":"On-device colour evaluation and best-effort heuristics. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://hued.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/hued-logo.png","coverKind":"logo","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":21,"narrative":{"headline":"One colour. [[A reason to look a little closer]].","scene":"Imagine being asked to make something creative today. A blank prompt is wide open; one findable colour gives your attention somewhere specific to go.","question":"How much structure helps creativity begin?","insight":"[[A small shared constraint]] gives the day a common starting point without prescribing the photograph.","pivot":"I connected the daily colour to finding, photographing, and receiving feedback, while keeping the photographs central.","takeaway":"A good prompt opens possibilities by making the first step small.","journey":[{"title":"Notice","detail":"Find the shared colour in the world."},{"title":"Capture","detail":"Make your own interpretation."},{"title":"Reflect","detail":"Use the feedback as guidance, with its limits visible."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the invitation specific.","Keep the creative result in the foreground.","Let a score explain rather than overrule."],"annotations":[{"title":"One daily invitation","detail":"The product identity anchors a shared colour challenge."},{"title":"Interpretation stays personal","detail":"Local scoring is feedback, not a verdict on creativity."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · daily prompt and scoring","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person looking for a small creative prompt","context":"Noticing everyday surroundings through one daily colour.","need":"Find a subject and share a personal interpretation.","friction":"Scores and streaks can turn exploration into pressure.","implication":"Let the photograph lead and keep feedback supportive."}},"theme":{"layout":"photojournal","mode":"light","paper":"#fffaf6","ink":"#2f2927","dim":"#75615b","line":"#e8d5ca","accent":"#ad4b35","wash":"#f8e2d7","bodyFont":"Montserrat","displayFont":"Montserrat","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A colour in the everyday","process":"A constraint with room for you","decisions":"Designing the daily invitation","outcome":"Notice, photograph, reflect","reflection":"Creativity before streaks"},"decisionHeading":"Make the daily prompt easy to act on.","resultHeading":"A little colour, interpreted differently."},"visual":{"presentation":"type","label":"Product direction","caption":"A daily photography prompt with a shared constraint, on-device scoring, and a warm visual language.","source":"Product scope and project documentation"},"nextStory":{"slug":"yuva-panaji","name":"Yuva Panaji","headline":"Connect a community’s public story to the work behind it."}},"yuva-panaji":{"slug":"yuva-panaji","name":"Yuva Panaji","kind":"product","headline":"Connect a community’s public story to the work behind it.","summary":"A community website and operations portal linking event discovery with registration and committee workflows.","role":"Information architecture, registration & volunteer workflows","status":"Working system","context":"Community operations · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"connected surfaces"},{"value":"Public","label":"events and registration"},{"value":"Private","label":"committee operations"}],"problem":"A community organisation needs a public account of what it does and a practical way to run the next event. The reviewed Yuva source brings those responsibilities together: public discovery and registration on one side, event administration and committee workflows on the other.","process":["I mapped the service from public event discovery and registration through committee review and the attendance desk. Participants and volunteers need different views of the same event information.","The design priority is continuity: information entered on a registration form should remain useful when a volunteer checks someone in, corrects a record, or prepares a printed list."],"decisions":[{"title":"Connect discovery to the event’s operational record","choice":"The public registration journey and admin roster belong to the same event model.","reason":"A committee should not have to reinterpret a public sign-up into a separate attendance workflow.","tradeoff":"Custom events need flexible fields without making every registration form equally complex."},{"title":"Give public and committee users different surfaces","choice":"Visitors see event and organisation information, while management workflows sit behind admin access.","reason":"The interface should reflect different responsibilities and information needs.","tradeoff":"A clean separation in navigation is only part of the requirement; permissions and data handling must be verified independently."},{"title":"Include the work that happens away from the screen","choice":"The documented system supports desk attendance sheets and spreadsheet exports.","reason":"Community operations often happen at an event desk, where a printable list can be more useful than another dashboard.","tradeoff":"Exported files can become stale. The workflow should explain when a printout was produced and how corrections return to the system."}],"outcome":"The resulting service connects public information, event registration, and committee operations. The UX contribution follows information into rosters, corrections, exports, and attendance work, extending the journey beyond the public webpage.","reflection":"The strongest contribution is continuity between a visitor’s action and a committee member’s next task. I would measure that continuity at real event handoffs, where duplicate entry, missing information, and stale lists become visible.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: follow one event from discovery through registration, roster correction, and check-in with both participant and committee perspectives.","Proposed measure: duplicate corrections, incomplete registrations, and handoff effort at the event desk, with a documented baseline."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/db.js","detail":"Current public/admin architecture, event operations and Supabase-backed data layer. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://yuva-website.netlify.app/","cover":"assets/products/yuva.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":24,"narrative":{"headline":"The event page is only [[the beginning of the work]].","scene":"Imagine someone finding an event and registering. Their next step is attendance. For the committee, that same action starts a chain of rosters, corrections, desk sheets, and follow-up.","question":"Can one action stay useful all the way to the event desk?","insight":"[[The public journey and the operational record]] are parts of the same service. The handoff matters as much as the page.","pivot":"I mapped how public discovery and registration continue into committee workflows, including the work that happens away from a screen.","takeaway":"Design the next person’s task into the current person’s action.","journey":[{"title":"Discover","detail":"Find the event and its details."},{"title":"Register","detail":"Submit the information the event actually needs."},{"title":"Prepare","detail":"Carry it into a usable committee roster."},{"title":"Check in","detail":"Support attendance at the event desk."}],"decisionLeads":["Connect the action to the record behind it.","Give each responsibility its own surface.","Design for the handoff beyond the screen."],"annotations":[{"title":"A public front door","detail":"The community website introduces the organisation and its work."},{"title":"An operational continuation","detail":"The case follows the event into registration and committee tasks."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · public and admin workflows","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Event participant & committee volunteer","context":"One person registers; another prepares the roster and attendance desk.","need":"Carry the same event information from discovery into check-in.","friction":"Public sign-ups and operational lists can become disconnected.","implication":"Design the participant and volunteer journeys together."}},"theme":{"layout":"service","mode":"light","paper":"#fbfaf5","ink":"#22301f","dim":"#5c6855","line":"#d8ddce","accent":"#157a45","wash":"#e3eedc","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Two sides of one event","process":"Registration continues at the desk","decisions":"Designing the whole service","outcome":"From public page to roster","reflection":"Following real event work"},"decisionHeading":"Connect public events to the work behind them.","resultHeading":"The public event and the work behind it."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/yuva.webp","alt":"Yuva Panaji — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of Yuva Panaji. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"deckforge","name":"DeckForge","headline":"Recover a deck’s structure, then make it useful again."}},"deckforge":{"slug":"deckforge","name":"DeckForge","kind":"product","headline":"Recover a deck’s structure, then make it useful again.","summary":"A browser presentation tool that turns PDF drawing instructions into editable objects, animation, and presenting workflows.","role":"Product idea, editing experience & presentation flows","status":"Working product","context":"Presentation tools · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Editable","label":"reconstructed page objects"},{"value":"40","label":"documented animation presets"},{"value":"Browser","label":"core document processing"}],"problem":"A PDF preserves how a slide looks but usually loses the convenient objects a presenter wants to edit. DeckForge approaches that gap as a reconstruction problem: recover meaningful text, images, and vector objects, then let a person work with them rather than repainting a screenshot.","process":["I framed the editor around objects a presenter can recognise: a text block, a logo, a shape, or a chart. A technically selectable fragment is not useful if its meaning is unclear.","The flow needs to explain what can be edited, preserve a usable slide when recovery is limited, and distinguish live presentation pacing from an exported recording."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make layers correspond to recognisable objects","choice":"Paths are grouped into likely logos, dividers, shapes, and chart series, while text is reconstructed into blocks.","reason":"An editable layer is useful only if the person recognises what it represents.","tradeoff":"Object recovery is heuristic. Preserving appearance can be more valuable than forcing misleading editability."},{"title":"Let a presenter control the pace","choice":"Animations can wait for an advance action, while timed outputs play sequentially.","reason":"A live talk and an exported recording need different behaviour from the same slide.","tradeoff":"That difference must be visible before export so the presenter knows what an audience will receive."},{"title":"Keep the core document workflow local","choice":"PDF reconstruction and editing run in the browser; phone pairing has a separate connection mechanism.","reason":"Local processing supports a lightweight workflow and avoids requiring a document-upload service.","tradeoff":"Phone pairing still uses signalling, and some remote features exchange preview frames. The privacy explanation should distinguish those paths."}],"outcome":"The product connects recovered slide objects with editing, sequencing, presentation, and export. My UX focus is what someone can recognise and do with the recovered content, including the explanation of content that remains an image.","reflection":"The useful boundary is between faithful recovery and an honest fallback. A tool that explains why part of a page remains an image can be more trustworthy than one that claims every object was successfully recovered.","nextSteps":["Proposed benchmark: import a defined set of native-text, scanned, multi-column, and dense-vector PDFs; record editability and visible fidelity separately.","Proposed study: revise a real slide and present it, measuring manual corrections and surprises between editor, audience view, and exported output."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, How extraction works and Known limitations","detail":"Reconstruction pipeline, presentation model, export boundaries and remote-pairing caveat."},{"label":"src/engine and src/store","detail":"Shared extraction, animation, export and document-state behaviour. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":true,"liveUrl":"https://deckforge.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/deckforge.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":27,"narrative":{"headline":"The deck is a PDF. [[The idea is still editable]].","scene":"Imagine needing to revise one text block or animate one diagram, but having only the exported PDF. The visual is preserved; the convenient objects are gone.","question":"What does it take to recover something a presenter can actually work with?","insight":"[[Recognisable objects make editability useful]]. Text, paths, and images need to become meaningful layers rather than a pile of drawing instructions.","pivot":"I framed the journey around recognising an object, revising it, and presenting it, with an explanation when recovered content cannot be edited.","takeaway":"Recover enough structure to make the next action possible.","journey":[{"title":"Recover","detail":"Read text, paths, and images from the PDF."},{"title":"Group","detail":"Reassemble objects the person recognises."},{"title":"Revise","detail":"Edit and sequence meaningful layers."},{"title":"Present","detail":"Carry the same slide model into the talk."}],"decisionLeads":["Recover the object a person recognises.","Let the presenter control the pace.","Explain what stays local and what connects."],"annotations":[{"title":"A working reconstruction tool","detail":"The capture introduces the actual editor workflow."},{"title":"An honest fallback","detail":"Scanned or dense pages can remain usable raster layers."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · slide editing journey","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Presenter revising an existing PDF","context":"Making changes shortly before sharing or presenting a deck.","need":"Recognise editable content and control presentation pacing.","friction":"A recovered fragment may be selectable without being meaningful.","implication":"Expose recognisable objects and explain editing limits."}},"theme":{"layout":"slides","mode":"dark","paper":"#17191c","ink":"#f7f8fa","dim":"#b2bac6","line":"#545c67","accent":"#b9aeff","wash":"#302b46","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Inter","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A deck that needs changing","process":"Objects should be recognisable","decisions":"Designing edit and present","outcome":"The recovered slide","reflection":"When a page stays an image"},"decisionHeading":"Make the PDF’s editable parts understandable.","resultHeading":"A slide that can keep moving."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/deckforge.webp","alt":"DeckForge — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of DeckForge. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1269,"height":714},"nextStory":{"slug":"vansh-vriksha","name":"Vansh Vriksha","headline":"Make family connections explorable, and their sources visible."}},"vansh-vriksha":{"slug":"vansh-vriksha","name":"Vansh Vriksha","kind":"product","headline":"Make family connections explorable, and their sources visible.","summary":"A community genealogy archive that treats relationships, provenance, and contribution review as parts of the same experience.","role":"Archive concept, relationship exploration & contribution UX","status":"Working archive","context":"Community knowledge · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Graph","label":"relationship model"},{"value":"Provenance","label":"per-record intent"},{"value":"Reviewed","label":"contribution workflow"}],"problem":"A family archive is not simply a diagram of names. Relationships can be disputed, spellings vary, and historical records represent some people much better than others. Vansh Vriksha is structured around exploring those connections while preserving the basis for a record.","process":["I treated the archive as a set of connected records with several entry points: a person, a family, a village, or a relationship. The UX should help someone follow a connection without losing its source.","The demonstration content lets the experience be explored; it is not verified family evidence. Missing records, uncertain relationships, and contribution review need visible explanations."],"decisions":[{"title":"Model relationships beyond a simple tree","choice":"The graph includes family unions and parent links rather than forcing every connection into one hierarchy.","reason":"A plain tree can misrepresent remarriage and half-sibling relationships. The data model needs to preserve the actual relationship.","tradeoff":"A more faithful model is harder to lay out and explain, especially on small screens."},{"title":"Keep a record’s basis alongside the record","choice":"The documented model includes provenance and versioned family files.","reason":"Someone reading a family claim should have a way to understand where it came from and how it changed.","tradeoff":"A source field does not guarantee accuracy. Disagreement and corrections still need a human review process."},{"title":"Separate contribution from publication","choice":"Visitors can submit without an account, while a maintainer reviews additions.","reason":"A low-friction contribution path can coexist with an accountable published archive.","tradeoff":"Moderation becomes an operational commitment; the interface should communicate what happens while a submission waits."}],"outcome":"The archive connects relationship exploration, sourced records, and contribution review. My UX focus is helping someone understand a connection and its basis. The demonstration dataset exercises the journey without establishing verified family claims.","reflection":"The design is strongest when it makes the limits of the archive legible. Missing branches and uneven historical coverage should be visible context, not silently mistaken for missing people or social importance.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: ask a family contributor to submit a relationship, inspect its provenance, and correct a disputed connection.","Proposed review: distinguish demonstration records from sourced records in every public count and assess living-person privacy across search and sharing."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and src/lib/archive.ts","detail":"Versioned record storage, graph exploration, moderation, demonstration data and known biases. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://vanshvriksha.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/vansh-live.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":30,"narrative":{"headline":"A name is one record. [[A family is a set of connections]].","scene":"Imagine recognising a relative in an archive and wanting to know how the relationship was recorded. The diagram can show a connection, but it also needs a basis you can inspect.","question":"How can an archive invite contribution without pretending every claim is settled?","insight":"[[Provenance belongs with the relationship]]. A family graph, versioned records, and review make different parts of that promise concrete.","pivot":"I connected exploration with source-aware records and a contribution path that separates submitting from publishing.","takeaway":"An archive should show what it knows and how it came to know it.","journey":[{"title":"Explore","detail":"Follow the connections through the archive."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Read the basis for a record."},{"title":"Contribute","detail":"Submit a correction or a family link."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Publish after a maintainer’s review."}],"decisionLeads":["Model the relationship faithfully.","Keep the basis of a claim close.","Let a contribution wait for review."],"annotations":[{"title":"Multiple ways into a family history","detail":"The public experience offers search and relationship exploration."},{"title":"Coverage stays conditional","detail":"Demonstration records and historical coverage limits need context."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · graph and review model","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Family member exploring a connection","context":"Following names and relationships with incomplete historical information.","need":"Understand a relationship and the evidence behind it.","friction":"A convincing diagram can imply certainty or completeness.","implication":"Keep sources and contribution review near the record."}},"theme":{"layout":"archive","mode":"light","paper":"#fbf6ec","ink":"#37271f","dim":"#78664e","line":"#ddcfb6","accent":"#a8322a","wash":"#f6e2dc","bodyFont":"Archivo","displayFont":"Newsreader","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Following a family connection","process":"A connection needs a source","decisions":"Designing a careful archive","outcome":"The record and its relationships","reflection":"What the archive leaves out"},"decisionHeading":"Keep the evidence behind a relationship visible.","resultHeading":"A family connection with its context intact."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/vansh-live.webp","alt":"Vansh Vriksha — archived product interface","caption":"The original archive interface introduces people, families and the connections between records.","label":"Archive interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"premoney","name":"PreMoney","headline":"Make automatic expense tracking correctable."}},"premoney":{"slug":"premoney","name":"PreMoney","kind":"product","headline":"Make automatic expense tracking correctable.","summary":"A personal-finance app that converts bank and UPI messages into reviewable transactions, categories, and summaries.","role":"Product concept, transaction review & correction flows","status":"Interactive app","context":"Personal finance · Working product","facts":[{"value":"SMS","label":"transaction input"},{"value":"Confidence","label":"visible in parser model"},{"value":"Review","label":"unresolved merchants"}],"problem":"Manual expense entry asks people to repeat information already present in transaction alerts. PreMoney explores a more useful starting point: extract likely transactions from messages, then make uncertainty and correction part of the workflow.","process":["I framed the core journey as evidence, suggested transaction, review, correction, and summary. A person should be able to understand why an alert became a record and recover when the interpretation is wrong.","Privacy explanations belong alongside optional categorisation and synchronisation. The experience needs to make those choices understandable without relying on a blanket claim that all processing stays on the device."],"decisions":[{"title":"Prefer an uncertain result to a fabricated detail","choice":"The parser lowers confidence when a field is uncertain and records when a merchant is unresolved.","reason":"A plausible but incorrect financial record can be harder to notice than a clear request for review.","tradeoff":"Conservative parsing can miss legitimate alerts. The product needs a recoverable route for false rejections."},{"title":"Make correction useful beyond a single transaction","choice":"Transaction editing and review make suggested categories and corrections inspectable.","reason":"Repeated mistakes should become easier to diagnose than manually editing the same pattern forever.","tradeoff":"A learned rule can also propagate a wrong assumption, so its effect must remain inspectable."},{"title":"Treat enrichment as a separate data decision","choice":"Optional categorisation and synchronisation need a separate explanation from reading a transaction alert.","reason":"People need an understandable account of which data is used by which feature.","tradeoff":"The product documentation describes external processing paths; disclosures and controls need review before making privacy promises."}],"outcome":"The app connects message evidence, suggested transactions, review, correction, and reports. My UX focus is an inspectable financial record with a recoverable route for uncertainty. Transaction accuracy remains a question for a defined evaluation.","reflection":"The important design question is how gracefully automation admits uncertainty. I would prioritise correcting a wrong financial entry and understanding its origin before adding more summary charts.","nextSteps":["Proposed benchmark: use a consented, redacted alert corpus to measure false accepts, false rejects, duplicate errors, and corrections by bank format.","Proposed release review: document the external-processing and sync paths, then test whether a person understands and controls them."],"sources":[{"label":"lib/domain/sms/sms_parser.dart and lib/domain/categorize","detail":"Confidence model, rejection reasons and merchant categorisation."},{"label":"lib/domain/ai/groq_service.dart and lib/ui/settings/sms_sandbox_sheet.dart","detail":"External enrichment payload and diagnostic review surface. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/PreMoney-releases","cover":"assets/products/premoney.svg","coverKind":"title","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":33,"narrative":{"headline":"The app found a transaction. [[Can you trust the number?]]","scene":"Imagine an expense app reading a bank alert. Automation has saved an entry—but if it guessed the amount or merchant, the summary now looks more certain than its input.","question":"How should automatic tracking admit that it is unsure?","insight":"[[A reviewable uncertainty is useful information]]. Confidence, rejection reasons, and unresolved merchants give correction a place in the workflow.","pivot":"I framed the journey around inspecting and correcting a suggested transaction, with separate explanations for optional categorisation and sync.","takeaway":"Automation earns trust by making its uncertainty correctable.","journey":[{"title":"Read","detail":"Distinguish an account movement from other alerts."},{"title":"Flag","detail":"Keep uncertainty in the transaction model."},{"title":"Correct","detail":"Inspect the origin and improve the record."}],"decisionLeads":["Leave uncertainty visible.","Make a correction useful beyond one row.","Explain enrichment as its own data decision."],"annotations":[{"title":"Confidence is a design input","detail":"Ambiguous transaction alerts need an explicit review path before a record feels trustworthy."},{"title":"Correction has a home","detail":"The app includes transaction editing and review."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · transaction confidence and review","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person reviewing everyday spending","context":"Using transaction alerts to understand money movement.","need":"Trust the record enough to review and correct it.","friction":"An ambiguous alert can become a plausible but incorrect transaction.","implication":"Make uncertainty visible and correction recoverable."}},"theme":{"layout":"statement","mode":"light","paper":"#f4f6f3","ink":"#161a18","dim":"#59665e","line":"#d4ded6","accent":"#16745f","wash":"#dcefe8","bodyFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","displayFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A record you need to trust","process":"Uncertainty should stay visible","decisions":"Review before confidence","outcome":"The transaction journey","reflection":"Evaluating the mistakes"},"decisionHeading":"Make uncertain transactions easy to review.","resultHeading":"A suggested transaction, open to review."},"visual":{"presentation":"type","label":"Product direction","caption":"A personal-finance app that converts bank and UPI messages into reviewable transactions, categories, and summaries.","source":"Product scope and project documentation"},"nextStory":{"slug":"soundbox","name":"SoundBox","headline":"Let the song wait for the learner."}},"soundbox":{"slug":"soundbox","name":"SoundBox","kind":"product","headline":"Let the song wait for the learner.","summary":"A piano-learning app that connects familiar songs, visible keys, and listening-based progression at the player’s pace.","role":"Learning concept, practice flow & feedback design","status":"Working app","context":"Music learning · Working product","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"arrangement levels"},{"value":"Wait mode","label":"default practice rhythm"},{"value":"On-device","label":"audio processing"}],"problem":"Someone who wants to play a familiar song may not yet read notation or keep up with a timed lesson. SoundBox starts from the song and a picture of the keyboard, then waits for the player to produce the expected note or chord.","process":["I organised the learning experience around a song, an approachable arrangement, and a repeatable passage. The default practice mode waits for the learner instead of making the person chase a clock.","Listening feedback needs a clear ready, heard, and retry state. Recognition reliability has not been established across real instruments and rooms, so recovery deserves as much design attention as successful progression."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make progression depend on readiness","choice":"The default mode advances after the expected musical input rather than at a fixed tempo.","reason":"A beginner can concentrate on finding the right movement without constantly falling behind the interface.","tradeoff":"Recognition errors can still interrupt the experience, so recovery and a clear input state are essential."},{"title":"Offer more than one way into a song","choice":"Chord, simplified, and full-melody arrangements support different levels of challenge.","reason":"A learner can begin with something recognisable and increase complexity when ready.","tradeoff":"An automatically simplified arrangement still needs musical review; lower note density is not always easier to play."},{"title":"Support repetition without punishment","choice":"Section looping and restrained progress mechanics accompany local practice history.","reason":"Practice often means repeating one difficult passage, not completing a song from the beginning.","tradeoff":"Streaks and XP can distract from learning if they reward activity without meaningful progress."}],"outcome":"The app makes paced practice, alternative arrangements, repetition, and progress visible. My design focus is letting a learner stay with a difficult passage and continue when ready. Recognition reliability and learning outcomes remain to be evaluated.","reflection":"The strongest interaction is the absence of pressure from a clock. I would evaluate the complete loop—hear the song, locate a note, play, understand feedback, and retry—before judging the app from completion counts.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: observe a beginner learning one short passage with the default wait mode, including how they recover from missed detections.","Proposed benchmark: test recognised notes and chords under varied room noise, microphones, and keyboards; report false advances separately from missed inputs."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, How the listening works and Practising","detail":"Arrangements, paced learning, recognition approach, local processing and practice loops. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/soundbox","cover":"assets/products/soundbox.svg","coverKind":"title","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":36,"narrative":{"headline":"The next note can [[wait for you]].","scene":"Imagine knowing the song you want to play, but being unable to read a page of notation or keep up with a timed lesson. Finding the right key already takes attention.","question":"What changes when the lesson follows the learner’s pace?","insight":"[[Removing the clock changes the interaction]]. The default practice loop waits for the expected note or chord, with space to retry.","pivot":"I connected a visible keyboard, familiar songs, and listening-based progression to make the next musical action understandable.","takeaway":"A learning tool should make another attempt feel possible.","journey":[{"title":"See","detail":"Know which key or chord comes next."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Try it without racing a timer."},{"title":"Listen","detail":"Let the app recognise the input."},{"title":"Repeat","detail":"Loop the difficult passage when useful."}],"decisionLeads":["Let readiness decide the next step.","Offer more than one way into the song.","Support repetition without punishment."],"annotations":[{"title":"A paced practice loop","detail":"The current chord, visible keyboard and Wait for me control make the next attempt clear."},{"title":"Recognition has limits","detail":"Real room and instrument conditions remain evaluation work."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · wait-mode practice","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Beginner practising a song","context":"Repeating a difficult passage at a comfortable pace.","need":"Find the notes and continue when ready.","friction":"A fixed tempo or unclear listening feedback can interrupt practice.","implication":"Wait for the learner and make retry states clear."}},"theme":{"layout":"practice","mode":"dark","paper":"#0f1017","ink":"#f2f3f8","dim":"#b8bfd1","line":"#42495f","accent":"#b8aaff","wash":"#302b4b","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Nunito","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A difficult passage","process":"The learner sets the pace","decisions":"Feedback without a race","outcome":"The practice loop","reflection":"Listening in real conditions"},"decisionHeading":"Let the learner set the pace.","resultHeading":"Stay with the passage. Continue when ready."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/soundbox-practice.png","alt":"Original SoundBox practice screen with chord sequence, keyboard and Wait for me mode.","caption":"The practice screen keeps the current chord, keyboard and pace controls together.","label":"Practice interface","source":"SoundBox/docs/play-screen.png","focus":[{"title":"Recognise","detail":"The current chord and keyboard remain visible."},{"title":"Repeat","detail":"A passage can be approached at the learner’s pace."},{"title":"Control the pace","detail":"Wait for me is an explicit mode."}],"width":1080,"height":2400,"details":[{"decision":0,"x":25,"y":1470,"width":1030,"height":700,"caption":"The keyboard and Wait for me control sit together in the practice screen.","alt":"Detail of the original SoundBox keyboard, listening button and Wait for me practice mode."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"wheelie","name":"Wheelie","headline":"Coordinate the ride before adding more to the rider’s attention."}},"wheelie":{"slug":"wheelie","name":"Wheelie","kind":"product","headline":"Coordinate the ride before adding more to the rider’s attention.","summary":"A group-riding beta that connects rendezvous planning, rider location, helmet voice, and ride summaries.","role":"Service idea, coordination journeys & interaction constraints","status":"Beta · accessibility polish in progress","context":"Group mobility · Working product","facts":[{"value":"3","label":"documented product pillars"},{"value":"64px+","label":"primary target intent"},{"value":"Beta","label":"not a safety validation"}],"problem":"Group riding begins before everyone reaches the same road. People start in different places, need a meeting point, and can lose contact after setting off. Wheelie brings planning, group location, and helmet voice into one service concept, with large, simple controls as an explicit design constraint.","process":["I framed three moments: meeting before a ride, coordinating with the group, and looking back afterwards. Each moment needs a different level of detail and attention.","The constrained physical context informs large targets, restrained choices, and clear connection states. The next evaluation belongs in stationary or controlled settings; the beta is not evidence of safe in-motion interaction."],"decisions":[{"title":"Begin with a rendezvous, not just a destination","choice":"The service computes a meeting point for people starting from different places.","reason":"The group’s coordination problem exists before shared navigation can begin.","tradeoff":"A fair computed point may not be a suitable physical stopping place; people need to review the suggestion."},{"title":"Reduce reliance on precise gestures","choice":"The design calls for large primary targets and simple tap/drag interactions.","reason":"A constrained physical context should affect the interface model from the start.","tradeoff":"Large buttons alone cannot establish safe in-motion use. Evaluation should begin in stationary or controlled settings."},{"title":"Separate coordination from reflection","choice":"Live map and voice support the current group; ride summaries serve the post-ride story.","reason":"Different moments deserve different information and levels of attention.","tradeoff":"Social summaries can create incentives around speed; the content priorities deserve review before wider use."}],"outcome":"The beta brings meeting points, group location, voice, and ride summaries into one service. My UX focus separates preparation, coordination, and reflection. Accessibility polish and controlled evaluation of interaction demands remain next steps.","reflection":"The next design decision is what to remove from the active-ride surface. Good coordination should reduce interaction demands, with planning and richer controls handled while stopped.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: test rendezvous planning and headset controls while stationary, then assess attention demands under an appropriately controlled protocol.","Proposed checks: poor connectivity, location permission changes, unavailable voice, battery use, and clarity about whether rider positions are stale."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README and design.md","detail":"Product pillars, beta status, target-size intent and unfinished accessibility/glove review. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/wheelie","cover":"assets/products/wheelie.svg","coverKind":"title","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":39,"narrative":{"headline":"Before riding together, [[everyone has to meet]].","scene":"Imagine a group starting from different parts of a city. Sharing a destination does not yet answer where to meet or how to stay coordinated once the ride begins.","question":"Which coordination decisions belong before the group is moving?","insight":"[[The ride starts with a rendezvous]]. Planning, rider location, and helmet voice solve different moments of the same service.","pivot":"I connected those moments while treating attention, large controls, and clear connection states as design constraints.","takeaway":"Good coordination should ask for less attention at the busiest moment.","journey":[{"title":"Plan","detail":"Review a suitable meeting point while stopped."},{"title":"Coordinate","detail":"Know who is where and whether the information is current."},{"title":"Reflect","detail":"Tell the ride’s story after it is over."}],"decisionLeads":["Solve the meeting before the navigation.","Reduce the precision an action requires.","Keep the ride and its later story separate."],"annotations":[{"title":"A connected service","detail":"The three pillars cover meeting, communication, and ride summaries."},{"title":"A beta with evaluation ahead","detail":"Accessibility and glove polish remain in progress."}],"evidenceTag":"Beta artifact · controlled evaluation next","tone":"rust","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Ride organiser coordinating a group","context":"Preparing meeting points and keeping a group in contact.","need":"Review a rendezvous and understand group connection status.","friction":"Different starting places and physical interaction constraints complicate coordination.","implication":"Prioritise preparation, large targets, and clear connection feedback."}},"theme":{"layout":"rendezvous","mode":"light","paper":"#f7f9ff","ink":"#1d2a41","dim":"#5b6b84","line":"#d2dcee","accent":"#0b57b4","wash":"#e2ecff","bodyFont":"Roboto","displayFont":"Roboto","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Before the group sets off","process":"Coordination comes first","decisions":"Designing for the context","outcome":"A ride in three moments","reflection":"Controlled evaluation next"},"decisionHeading":"Change the interface around the ride.","resultHeading":"Prepare together. Coordinate clearly. Reflect later."},"visual":{"presentation":"type","label":"Product direction","caption":"A group-riding beta that connects rendezvous planning, rider location, helmet voice, and ride summaries.","source":"Product scope and project documentation"},"nextStory":{"slug":"matinee","name":"Matinee","headline":"Help a film list become a choice."}},"matinee":{"slug":"matinee","name":"Matinee","kind":"product","headline":"Help a film list become a choice.","summary":"A cinema-tracking product connecting discovery, a personal watchlist, ratings, and locally generated recommendations.","role":"Discovery concept, browsing journeys & responsive UX","status":"Interactive app","context":"Entertainment discovery · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"media types: films and series"},{"value":"Local","label":"recommendation scoring"},{"value":"Adaptive","label":"phone and wider layouts"}],"problem":"Finding a title and choosing what to watch are related but different tasks. Matinee connects browsing with a personal record of saved and watched titles, using that history to shape the next set of suggestions.","process":["I connected browsing with the personal record of saved, rated, and watched titles. The design question is how that history can make the next discovery session more relevant without becoming repetitive.","Language preferences, variety, and recognisable film/series labels inform the browsing experience. Navigation and poster density should adapt to the screen while keeping the selected title and next action clear."],"decisions":[{"title":"Keep a title’s type visible","choice":"Movie/series identifiers appear in discovery and combined lists.","reason":"A person choosing a two-hour film has a different commitment from someone starting a series.","tradeoff":"A badge helps scanning but does not replace runtime and season information near a decision."},{"title":"Let personal history influence discovery","choice":"Ratings and watch patterns feed a local scoring process, with controls against repetitive franchises or genres.","reason":"Recommendations should reflect preference without becoming a list of near-duplicates.","tradeoff":"A scoring rule is not evidence of relevance. Cold-start and unexpected recommendations need separate evaluation."},{"title":"Adapt the structure to the screen","choice":"Phone navigation becomes a side rail at wider sizes, and poster grids expand.","reason":"The same task can use space differently without turning a larger screen into a stretched phone layout.","tradeoff":"Density should still preserve readable text, meaningful focus order, and a clear selected title."}],"outcome":"The app connects discovery, saved titles, watch history, and ratings. My UX focus carries that personal context into the next choice while leaving room for language preferences and variety. Recommendation relevance has not been measured with a defined method.","reflection":"I would evaluate whether someone can make a satisfying choice, not whether a recommendation merely receives a click. Familiarity and diversity need to be judged together.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: compare a new profile with a rated-history profile and ask participants why each suggestion feels relevant or repetitive.","Proposed measure: useful selections, unwanted repetition, and confidence choosing across preferred languages."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README","detail":"Current mobile architecture, adaptive layout, watchlist rules and local recommendation factors. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://matinee.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/matinee.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":42,"narrative":{"headline":"A long watchlist. [[What are you watching tonight?]]","scene":"Imagine having plenty of saved titles and still spending the evening deciding. Discovery only becomes useful when it helps a person make a choice that fits.","question":"How can personal history help without filling the feed with more of the same?","insight":"[[Preference and variety need to work together]]. Ratings can guide the next suggestions, while repetition and language choices deserve their own controls.","pivot":"I connected watching, rating, and discovery into a loop, with a layout that adapts to how much room the person has.","takeaway":"A recommendation earns its place by helping someone choose.","journey":[{"title":"Save","detail":"Keep the titles that interest you."},{"title":"Watch & rate","detail":"Give your history useful preference signals."},{"title":"Discover","detail":"Bring that context back into the next choice."}],"decisionLeads":["Make the commitment visible.","Let the history influence the next screen.","Use the screen’s space deliberately."],"annotations":[{"title":"Discovery and tracking connect","detail":"The archived capture shows the browsing surface."},{"title":"Relevance is an evaluation question","detail":"Local scoring makes the approach concrete without establishing accuracy."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · history-to-discovery loop","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Viewer deciding what to watch next","context":"Balancing a personal watch history with a desire for something different.","need":"Choose a relevant title without repeatedly seeing near-duplicates.","friction":"A large catalogue creates more browsing without necessarily creating confidence.","implication":"Connect history with language, variety, and title context."}},"theme":{"layout":"cinema","mode":"dark","paper":"#0a0a0c","ink":"#f3f4f6","dim":"#b1b3c0","line":"#3d3541","accent":"#ff8eb5","wash":"#3e2030","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Outfit","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"Something worth watching","process":"History without repetition","decisions":"Designing the next choice","outcome":"A personal discovery loop","reflection":"Finding relevance in practice"},"decisionHeading":"Help someone choose what to watch.","resultHeading":"A viewing history that leads somewhere new."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/matinee.webp","alt":"Matinee — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of Matinee. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"nextStory":{"slug":"birthsky","name":"BirthSky","headline":"Turn a personal moment into a sky you can keep."}},"birthsky":{"slug":"birthsky","name":"BirthSky","kind":"product","headline":"Turn a personal moment into a sky you can keep.","summary":"An astronomy-inspired experience that turns a time and place into a customisable star-map wallpaper.","role":"Experience concept, input journey & visual direction","status":"Working browser project","context":"Personalised visualisation · Working product","facts":[{"value":"Time + place","label":"core input"},{"value":"3","label":"documented visual modes"},{"value":"2","label":"wallpaper formats"}],"problem":"A star map can be a technical chart or a personal object. BirthSky connects a simple biographical prompt to a visual reveal, then gives the person control over labels and the final wallpaper format.","process":["I organised the experience into a familiar prompt, a personal reveal, optional exploration, and a keepsake. Name, date, time, and place give the abstract sky a recognisable starting point.","The reveal needs understandable inputs. Unknown birth times, time zones, and any collection of personal details deserve clear explanations before a precise-looking image encourages confidence."],"decisions":[{"title":"Begin with a recognisable personal question","choice":"A short form collects the time and location before revealing the sky.","reason":"The input makes an otherwise abstract astronomical chart meaningful to the person requesting it.","tradeoff":"Unknown birth times and historical time zones require careful explanation; a precise-looking map can still rest on uncertain inputs."},{"title":"Make annotation a choice","choice":"The result supports alternative modes and toggles for constellation lines and names.","reason":"A person may want to learn from the sky or simply use it as a visual keepsake.","tradeoff":"Switches need clear effects without making the result feel like a complex scientific console."},{"title":"Design the output as part of the journey","choice":"Phone and desktop wallpaper downloads follow the reveal.","reason":"A useful result should fit where the person intends to use it.","tradeoff":"Export must preserve legibility and composition across aspect ratios rather than just scale one screenshot."}],"outcome":"The browser experience connects personal inputs with a customisable sky and wallpaper exports. My UX focus carries someone from a familiar question to a result they can keep. Input uncertainty and data-handling explanations remain priorities for refinement.","reflection":"The emotional reveal works best when the inputs remain understandable. Today I would put birth-time uncertainty, time-zone handling, and any collection of personal inputs ahead of additional visual modes.","nextSteps":["Proposed validation: compare known dates and locations with an authoritative sky reference, including historical daylight-saving and time-zone cases.","Proposed iteration: explain any logging before submission and make uncertain birth-time choices visible in the final result."],"sources":[{"label":"StarMap/index.html and js/app.js","detail":"Input flow, result controls, modes, export entry points and configured logging integration."},{"label":"StarMap/js/astronomy.js and js/exporter.js","detail":"Coordinate calculations and wallpaper generation. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://birthsky.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/birthsky.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":45,"narrative":{"headline":"A moment in your life. [[A sky you can keep]].","scene":"Imagine choosing a meaningful date and place, then seeing the sky interpreted as something personal. The experience has to connect a precise-looking visual to understandable inputs.","question":"How can a technical chart become a meaningful personal object?","insight":"[[The reveal and the keepsake are one journey]]. Time and place shape the view; annotation and aspect ratio shape how it is kept.","pivot":"I connected the personal prompt to a customisable sky and wallpaper outputs.","takeaway":"Make the inputs understandable enough to trust the reveal.","journey":[{"title":"Choose","detail":"Enter the time and location."},{"title":"Reveal","detail":"See the sky rendered from those inputs."},{"title":"Make it yours","detail":"Choose labels and a visual mode."},{"title":"Keep","detail":"Export for a phone or desktop."}],"decisionLeads":["Begin with a personal question.","Let annotation become a choice.","Design the output as part of the experience."],"annotations":[{"title":"A personal opening","detail":"The original product connects a biographical prompt to a sky view."},{"title":"A result beyond the page","detail":"Wallpaper exports give the reveal a place to live."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · input, render, export","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person creating a personal sky keepsake","context":"Entering a meaningful date and place, then choosing a wallpaper.","need":"Understand the inputs and keep a result that feels personal.","friction":"Uncertain times can produce a precise-looking picture.","implication":"Explain uncertainty and make visual annotation optional."}},"theme":{"layout":"constellation","mode":"dark","paper":"#030311","ink":"#e8e6f0","dim":"#b5b1ca","line":"#48415d","accent":"#d9bc73","wash":"#31291b","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Cormorant Garamond","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A moment that matters","process":"The reveal starts with the input","decisions":"Designing the keepsake","outcome":"A sky you can keep","reflection":"What an unknown time changes"},"decisionHeading":"Make the date, place and reveal understandable.","resultHeading":"A personal moment, made into a keepsake."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/birthsky.webp","alt":"BirthSky — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of BirthSky. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"scalesee","name":"ScaleSee","headline":"Make a large number feel like a real difference."}},"scalesee":{"slug":"scalesee","name":"ScaleSee","kind":"product","headline":"Make a large number feel like a real difference.","summary":"A visual comparison tool that turns quantities into scenes while keeping the meaning of scale explicit.","role":"Visual explanation, comparison journeys & interaction design","status":"Working browser product","context":"Quantitative explanation · Working product","facts":[{"value":"11","label":"categories listed in the source"},{"value":"2","label":"scale modes"},{"value":"Shareable","label":"comparison URLs"}],"problem":"A ratio can be mathematically clear and still be difficult to picture. ScaleSee turns two quantities into a shared visual comparison, choosing a representation that fits the kind of quantity instead of using the same chart for everything.","process":["I selected a visual reference appropriate to each kind of quantity: a ground line for height, nesting for land, or a shared course for speed. The reference is part of the explanation.","An extreme ratio creates a useful design tension: showing true scale can make one quantity nearly disappear. A compressed alternative needs an explicit label that remains with a shared comparison or exported image."],"decisions":[{"title":"Choose the picture to fit the quantity","choice":"Height shares a ground line, land can be nested, and speed uses a common course.","reason":"A meaningful reference can make a ratio easier to interpret than a generic bar.","tradeoff":"Every visual metaphor introduces assumptions; those assumptions need to be stated where they affect the comparison."},{"title":"Keep scale transformations explicit","choice":"True scale and logarithmic scale are separate modes.","reason":"When a small quantity almost disappears, that can be the honest answer; a compressed alternative should declare the change.","tradeoff":"A log view can make a scene more legible while reducing intuitive proportionality."},{"title":"Let the comparison leave the tool","choice":"A URL carries the selected comparison and an image export preserves the current drawing.","reason":"An explanation is more useful when someone can bring it into the conversation that prompted it.","tradeoff":"An exported image needs labels and scale context because it no longer has interactive controls beside it."}],"outcome":"The tool offers quantity-specific comparisons, explicit scale modes, sharing, and image export. My design focus makes a numerical relationship inspectable and portable. Correct interpretation of extreme ratios remains a question for user evaluation.","reflection":"The design lesson is that legibility and proportional truth sometimes pull in different directions. The interface should help the reader understand which trade-off a view makes.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask readers to estimate the ratio before and after each view, including an extreme comparison and a logarithmic one.","Proposed check: confirm labels, units, assumptions, and scale mode survive in every shared or exported artifact."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, What’s in it and Notes on the numbers","detail":"Category inventory, visual metaphors, scale-mode distinction and sharing/export. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://scalesee.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/scalesee.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":48,"narrative":{"headline":"You can read the number. [[Can you picture the difference?]]","scene":"Imagine comparing a million with a billion. The arithmetic is simple; making the difference feel concrete is another task.","question":"When does making both quantities visible change the meaning of the picture?","insight":"[[Legibility and proportional truth can pull apart]]. True scale may make one quantity almost disappear; a logarithmic view needs to declare its transformation.","pivot":"I used category-specific scenes and explicit scale modes, with enough context to travel in a shared image.","takeaway":"An explanation should keep its assumptions visible when it leaves the tool.","journey":[{"title":"Compare","detail":"Choose quantities of the same kind."},{"title":"See","detail":"Use a scene that fits the quantity."},{"title":"Interpret","detail":"Understand the selected scale mode."},{"title":"Share","detail":"Carry the labels and context into the output."}],"decisionLeads":["Let the quantity choose the picture.","Make a transformed scale explicit.","Keep the explanation intact when shared."],"annotations":[{"title":"A scene for the quantity","detail":"The product gives a ratio a concrete visual reference."},{"title":"Context travels with it","detail":"Scale, units, and assumptions matter in an exported comparison."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · comparison and export","tone":"teal","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Person trying to explain a large difference","context":"Comparing two quantities for a conversation or illustration.","need":"Understand the ratio and share it without losing context.","friction":"Extreme differences make small values disappear or invite misleading compression.","implication":"Declare scale changes and preserve labels in exports."}},"theme":{"layout":"comparison","mode":"light","paper":"#f2f1f0","ink":"#19181c","dim":"#625f68","line":"#d3d0d7","accent":"#8b3fa8","wash":"#eddff2","bodyFont":"Inter Tight","displayFont":"Inter Tight","order":["brief","process","outcome","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A difference hard to picture","process":"The reference does the explaining","decisions":"Designing an honest comparison","outcome":"The ratio, made visible","reflection":"Checking what the reader sees"},"decisionHeading":"Choose a reference people can understand.","resultHeading":"Two quantities. One understandable relationship."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/scalesee.webp","alt":"ScaleSee — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of ScaleSee. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"where-would-i-be","name":"Where Would I Be…?","headline":"Give deep time one personal point of reference."}},"where-would-i-be":{"slug":"where-would-i-be","name":"Where Would I Be…?","kind":"product","headline":"Give deep time one personal point of reference.","summary":"An interactive globe that lets a chosen place move with a plate-motion model and makes the model’s limits part of the story.","role":"Experience framing, interaction sequence & spatial explanation","status":"Working browser product","context":"Scientific storytelling · Working product","facts":[{"value":"1","label":"place-led starting question"},{"value":"Progressive","label":"details on demand"},{"value":"Modelled","label":"extrapolation, not prediction"}],"problem":"Plate motion is slow enough to be invisible in everyday life and large enough to reshape a world over time. This project gives that scale a personal anchor: choose a place, move the clock, and follow the ground beneath it.","process":["I gave the opening one task: choose a place. A time control and a short result then introduce motion, with optional detail kept out of the main globe.","The visual story needs to carry its qualification with it. Constant-motion extrapolation can invite exploration, but the result should not read as a reconstruction or prediction of the continents."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give each stage one main task","choice":"Choose a place first, then explore time, with optional comparison later.","reason":"A clear sequence can make a complex subject approachable without presenting every control at once.","tradeoff":"Progressive disclosure should not hide the model’s most important qualification until after someone has formed a false impression."},{"title":"Protect the space where the answer is drawn","choice":"The design keeps the main globe clear and groups controls in a bottom dock.","reason":"The selected point and its path are the explanation; a floating panel over them would compete with the result.","tradeoff":"A dock must adapt to available height so it does not consume the same space on a small screen."},{"title":"Make the scientific boundary legible","choice":"The source describes plate movement as a constant-motion extrapolation with increasing limits over long horizons.","reason":"Personal storytelling can encourage exploration without claiming to know the future shape of continents.","tradeoff":"A smooth animation can still imply certainty, so the qualification belongs near the time result."}],"outcome":"The globe connects location selection, time exploration, optional layers, and comparison. My UX direction gives deep time a personal entry point while keeping the model boundary near the result. Understanding that distinction still needs evaluation.","reflection":"The interaction succeeds as a question someone can explore, not as a forecast. I would test whether the person leaves understanding both plate motion and why the long-term path is conditional.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask a visitor to describe what changed and what assumptions the model makes after a short exploration.","Proposed checks: narrow-screen globe visibility, reduced-motion behaviour, keyboard interaction, and whether uncertainty stays visible at extreme dates."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, The layout rule and How much to trust it","detail":"Interaction sequence, unobstructed visual space and constant-motion model limitations. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://wherewouldibe.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/wherewouldibe.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":51,"narrative":{"headline":"Choose your ground. [[Let time move it]].","scene":"Imagine following the patch of crust beneath a familiar place over a time span you cannot experience. One personal point of reference gives deep time somewhere to begin.","question":"How can a compelling journey keep the model’s limits in view?","insight":"[[A smooth animation is still conditional]]. Holding present plate velocities constant creates an extrapolation whose limits grow with the horizon.","pivot":"I kept the globe clear, began with a place, and let the time control reveal a concise answer before offering deeper detail.","takeaway":"Let the story invite exploration without turning a model into a prediction.","journey":[{"title":"Anchor","detail":"Start from one familiar location."},{"title":"Explore","detail":"Move the time control and follow its path."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"Read the result alongside the model’s assumptions."}],"decisionLeads":["Give the first screen one useful question.","Protect the space where the answer is drawn.","Keep the scientific boundary near the result."],"annotations":[{"title":"A personal anchor","detail":"The selected point connects a large timescale to a familiar location."},{"title":"Details on demand","detail":"The interface leaves the globe room to explain the path."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · model and interaction sequence","tone":"blue","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Curious visitor exploring deep time","context":"Using a familiar location to make plate movement tangible.","need":"Follow a place through time and understand what the model means.","friction":"A smooth moving globe can look more certain than an extrapolation is.","implication":"Keep the model qualification near the time result."}},"theme":{"layout":"deep-time","mode":"dark","paper":"#05050c","ink":"#efedf8","dim":"#b4b0cb","line":"#48435d","accent":"#3ddbff","wash":"#152f3b","bodyFont":"Space Grotesk","displayFont":"Bricolage Grotesque","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A familiar place, another time","process":"A personal anchor for deep time","decisions":"Designing the time journey","outcome":"The moving point","reflection":"Where the model stops"},"decisionHeading":"Keep each prediction’s limits visible.","resultHeading":"A point through time, with the model in view."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/wherewouldibe.webp","alt":"Where Would I Be…? — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of Where Would I Be…?. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1280,"height":720},"nextStory":{"slug":"the-blog","name":"The Blog","headline":"Make publishing feel like writing."}},"the-blog":{"slug":"the-blog","name":"The Blog","kind":"product","headline":"Make publishing feel like writing.","summary":"An editorial site with a browser-based publishing workflow for poems, essays, fiction, and serial writing.","role":"Editorial structure, reading experience & author workflow","status":"Working publishing system","context":"Publishing experience · Working product","facts":[{"value":"2","label":"reader and author workflows"},{"value":"Drafts","label":"explicit publishing control"},{"value":"Verse","label":"preserved line breaks"}],"problem":"A personal writing practice should not require a technical deployment ritual for every post. The Blog connects a reading experience with a browser editor that handles content, images, drafts, and publishing through a familiar form.","process":["I framed writing, reviewing, and publishing as separate commitments. The editor should support the author’s work without requiring technical knowledge of how a post reaches the website.","The reading experience follows the form of the writing: poetry keeps its line breaks, while topics and series help a reader continue. Publication feedback should make draft and live states easy to distinguish."],"decisions":[{"title":"Give publishing a clear state","choice":"Draft and published are separate states, with a deliberate release action.","reason":"Writing and releasing a piece are different commitments and should remain distinguishable.","tradeoff":"Publishing can take time. The author needs clear confirmation of what is saved and what is live."},{"title":"Let the content form affect the layout","choice":"Poetry preserves line breaks and an appropriate verse treatment, while ordinary articles retain a reading layout.","reason":"The way a piece is written can be part of its meaning.","tradeoff":"Formatting conventions must be discoverable in the editor rather than depend on hidden technical knowledge."},{"title":"Make ongoing writing navigable","choice":"Topics and series connect related pieces within the editorial structure.","reason":"A reader should be able to follow a thread without knowing the order it was published.","tradeoff":"The documented title-based series matching is convenient but implicit; a visible series field would make mistakes easier to avoid."}],"outcome":"The site connects a reading experience with an editorial publishing path. The UX contribution makes room for poems, essays, series, drafts, and release feedback, so the author can focus on the writing and the reader can follow it.","reflection":"The important shift is from making content fit the tool to making the tool respect the writing. I would next make publishing status and series membership more visible to the author.","nextSteps":["Proposed evaluation: publish a draft, update an existing post, and add a series instalment without using development tools.","Proposed measure: accidental publication, broken links after slug edits, and clarity about whether the latest save is live."],"sources":[{"label":"Repository README, Writing a post and Things worth knowing","detail":"Browser editor, draft/build publishing path, verse formatting and title-based series conventions. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://blogs.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/blogs.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":54,"narrative":{"headline":"A finished draft should [[feel close to publishing]].","scene":"Imagine finishing a poem and then having to switch from writing into a development workflow. The mechanics of publishing can become a reason to put the piece aside.","question":"What should the author have to think about after the writing is done?","insight":"[[Publishing state belongs in the editor]]. A draft, a save, and a live page are different moments that need clear feedback.","pivot":"I connected a browser editor with a content-aware reading experience, respecting verse and serial writing along the way.","takeaway":"The tool should respect the shape of the writing.","journey":[{"title":"Compose","detail":"Work through an editorial interface."},{"title":"Review","detail":"Keep draft status and content choices visible."},{"title":"Release","detail":"Carry the saved piece through the build into the reading experience."}],"decisionLeads":["Make publication a visible commitment.","Let the form of the writing matter.","Make the next piece easy to find."],"annotations":[{"title":"An editorial front door","detail":"The site introduces the writing in its own visual voice."},{"title":"A workflow behind it","detail":"The browser editor supports the ongoing publishing practice."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · editorial workflow","tone":"violet","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Writer publishing & reader continuing a piece","context":"An author moves from draft to release; a reader moves between related writing.","need":"Preserve the form of the writing and a clear route to the next piece.","friction":"Publication states and implicit series conventions can be difficult to understand.","implication":"Give drafts, verse, and series explicit structure."}},"theme":{"layout":"editorial","mode":"light","paper":"#faf8f3","ink":"#14130f","dim":"#5a564c","line":"#d7d0c0","accent":"#6b3fa0","wash":"#eee5f5","bodyFont":"Inter","displayFont":"Fraunces","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A draft is not a release","process":"The form is part of the writing","decisions":"Designing reader and author journeys","outcome":"Writing with a place to live","reflection":"Making publication legible"},"decisionHeading":"Make it easy to find and continue reading.","resultHeading":"A home for drafts, poems, and continuing stories."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/blogs.webp","alt":"The Blog — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of The Blog. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"office-games","name":"The Office Games","headline":"Make the next person’s turn obvious."}},"office-games":{"slug":"office-games","name":"The Office Games","kind":"product","headline":"Make the next person’s turn obvious.","summary":"A team-trivia experience exploring quick room entry, visible roles, and shared game state for a short social break.","role":"Social game concept, room journey & turn-taking UX","status":"Working project · trivia module","context":"Social play · Working product","facts":[{"value":"4","label":"letters in a room code"},{"value":"Team-based","label":"turn and score model"},{"value":"Explicit","label":"local or network mode"}],"problem":"A short team game loses momentum when people are unsure how to join or whose turn it is. The reviewed Office Games module centres on a simple room flow, a lobby, team roles, and a visible answering/stealing sequence.","process":["I mapped the reviewed trivia journey from joining a room to understanding teams, turns, answering, and stealing. The broader games catalogue is outside the evidence available for this case study.","The UX needs to explain the connection mode before a group commits to a game. Trying a local demonstration and joining colleagues on separate devices are different experiences."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make joining a small action","choice":"A four-letter room code and a player name provide a concise entry path.","reason":"A social break should spend more of its time playing than explaining setup.","tradeoff":"A short code still needs clear invalid-room and full-room handling, and does not establish secure identity."},{"title":"Expose responsibility in the game state","choice":"The interface distinguishes answering and stealing teams, active players, and scores.","reason":"People need to see who can act and why the round is waiting.","tradeoff":"Role-based interaction becomes confusing if waiting states or connection loss are not explained."},{"title":"Keep connection mode understandable","choice":"The setup distinguishes a local demonstration from connected play.","reason":"A demo can help someone try the game, but should not promise multi-device play it cannot provide.","tradeoff":"A connected game needs clear waiting, conflict, reconnection, and host-recovery states."}],"outcome":"The reviewed trivia module connects joining, teams, round state, scoring, and chat. My UX focus makes the social sequence and responsibility for the next action visible. Local demonstration and connected play need distinct expectations.","reflection":"The design opportunity is a shared understanding of what happens next. I would assess joining, waiting, and recovery as carefully as the answer-selection screen.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: run a short trivia session with several participants and record setup explanations, confused turns, and waiting time.","Proposed checks: separate-device joining, reconnection, duplicate actions, host departure, and a clearly labelled local demo mode."],"sources":[{"label":"src/App.jsx and src/gameService.js","detail":"Reviewed room/lobby/trivia journey, short codes, team state, local-storage mock service and Firebase path. Reviewed 15 October 2026."}],"figures":[],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://officegames.srinidhibhat.com/","cover":"assets/products/officegames.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":4,"pdfPage":57,"narrative":{"headline":"The room is ready. [[Whose turn is it?]]","scene":"Imagine a short team break being spent explaining how to join, who can answer, and why the game is waiting. The social rhythm depends on those transitions being obvious.","question":"What does the group need to understand at every handoff?","insight":"[[Shared state has to become shared understanding]]. Room entry, team roles, and the answering/stealing sequence all contribute to the pace.","pivot":"I focused the reviewed trivia module on a concise joining path and visible responsibility through each round.","takeaway":"A social game moves when everyone knows what happens next.","journey":[{"title":"Join","detail":"Use a short code and a player name."},{"title":"Orient","detail":"See the teams and who can act."},{"title":"Play","detail":"Follow the answering and stealing sequence."},{"title":"Continue","detail":"Keep scores and waiting states understandable."}],"decisionLeads":["Make joining a small action.","Show why a round is waiting.","Keep the connection promise clear."],"annotations":[{"title":"A social entry point","detail":"The product offers a quick route into a shared game."},{"title":"Reviewed scope: trivia","detail":"Local demo and network-backed play make different connection promises."}],"evidenceTag":"Product artifact · reviewed trivia module","tone":"gold","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Colleague joining a short group game","context":"Entering a room and learning a team role during an office break.","need":"Join quickly and know whose turn it is.","friction":"Waiting and role changes become confusing when the interface is silent.","implication":"Make room, role, and next action visible."}},"theme":{"layout":"playroom","mode":"dark","paper":"#17181c","ink":"#f8f9fa","dim":"#bdc2ce","line":"#495160","accent":"#ff94b7","wash":"#422336","bodyFont":"Outfit","displayFont":"Fredoka","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A short break to play","process":"Someone needs the next turn","decisions":"Designing the social rhythm","outcome":"Inside the trivia round","reflection":"What waiting feels like"},"decisionHeading":"Make the next turn clear to everyone.","resultHeading":"A room with a shared social rhythm."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/products/officegames.webp","alt":"The Office Games — archived product interface","caption":"An archived view of The Office Games. The case study examines the UX direction behind the experience.","label":"Product interface","source":"Existing portfolio product capture","width":1265,"height":712},"nextStory":{"slug":"forma","name":"Forma","headline":"Give every health reading its context."}},"forma":{"slug":"forma","name":"Forma","kind":"product","headline":"Give every health reading its context.","summary":"A native Android health app that brings activity, sleep and personal trends together while making sources, missing data and experimental estimates visible.","role":"Product concept, health-data journeys & interaction design","status":"Native Android app · experimental wellness estimates","context":"Personal health","facts":[{"value":"5","label":"main destinations"},{"value":"44","label":"native metric views"},{"value":"Recorded","label":"measurement times retained"}],"problem":"A watch can collect more measurements than a person can comfortably interpret. A number also changes meaning when it is old, incomplete, imported or estimated. I framed Forma around a daily overview that stays useful without making uncertain data look more reliable than it is.","process":["I organised the experience around five destinations: Today for a daily overview, Sleep for the recorded night, Move for activity and workouts, Trends for comparison, and Health for the measurement library. The native app is the product; the React version in the repository is a design preview.","The data constraints shaped the UX. Samsung Health must share records through Health Connect, permissions can be partial, and not every watch measurement is available. Connection setup therefore needs to explain availability rather than treat an empty reading as a failure or a zero.","The later function audit separated Devices from Your data, moved optional personal tools into settings, removed controls without a working purpose, and put formulas behind expandable details. The archived screens illustrate the native visual direction; the audit and current source establish the later navigation and scope. This is an artifact-based retrospective, without a participant study or clinical validation."],"decisions":[{"title":"Distinguish recorded, missing and estimated values","choice":"Keep source labels and measurement times attached to readings. Preserve gaps in histories, identify sample data, and distinguish sleeping heart rate from a recorded resting-heart-rate value.","reason":"A recent refresh does not mean a sensor reading is recent. People need to understand what a value represents before comparing it with yesterday or using it to interpret their day.","tradeoff":"These labels add density. The hierarchy has to keep essential provenance visible while leaving longer explanations available on demand."},{"title":"Let a score open into its explanation","choice":"Show the score and its contributing values first, then offer the calculation and diagnostics in an expandable sheet. Recovery requires recorded sleep and a usable baseline vital; missing inputs can leave the estimate unavailable.","reason":"An unexplained score can look authoritative. Showing its inputs makes the estimate inspectable and lets the interface explain why the same score can accompany different activity records.","tradeoff":"Transparency does not validate the model. Sleep, recovery and body-energy estimates remain experimental wellness interpretations, distinct from Samsung proprietary scores and medical assessment."},{"title":"Separate connecting a device from managing data","choice":"Devices leads with connection state and the next available action. Your data contains coverage, export, optional import and local reset. Targets and personal tools have their own settings paths.","reason":"Someone trying to connect a watch should not have to interpret a coverage report or navigate file-management controls before granting access.","tradeoff":"Separating the tasks adds destinations. Clear names and back paths matter, particularly when permissions are partial or a watch has not synced."}],"outcome":"Forma offers a native daily overview, dedicated sleep and movement views, inspectable histories and a searchable health library. The UX contribution is a hierarchy that connects an overview to the evidence behind it. The repository documents native and emulator checks; physical Samsung sharing remains device-dependent, and the score models are not clinically validated.","reflection":"The most useful simplification was giving each kind of detail a clear home. Connection help belongs with Devices; coverage belongs with Your data; a calculation belongs behind its score. I would next check whether people can explain an unavailable or older reading as confidently as a complete one.","nextSteps":["Proposed study: ask people to distinguish sample, shared, direct-watch, older and unavailable readings, then explain what an experimental recovery score does and does not mean.","Proposed device checks: follow granted, denied and partial-permission journeys on a Samsung phone and watch, including delayed sync, expired direct readings and corrected history.","Proposed measures: connection-task completion, mistaken interpretations of missing data, and success finding a score explanation or exporting records."],"sources":[{"label":"Forma README and native interface source","detail":"Reviewed 16 October 2026. Native Android scope, five destinations, Samsung Health/Health Connect integration, measurement timestamps and experimental score limits. The web project is a design preview."},{"label":"docs/DESIGN.md and docs/UX-AUDIT.md","detail":"The later 1.5 audit supersedes the older 46-measurement design note: 44 native metric views, distinct Devices/Your data flows, optional personal tools and expandable score explanations."},{"label":"Archived native captures · sample data","detail":"Today rings and vitals captures from the project output illustrate the native visual system. The Today capture predates the Health tab rename and personal-tool cleanup; the later Your data capture shows the separated data-management flow. Values are demonstration data."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-today.webp","alt":"Archived Forma native Today screen with demo activity rings and sample heart rate","caption":"Archived native Today screen with clearly marked demo values. This capture predates the later Health-tab and personal-tool cleanup."},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-data.webp","alt":"Forma Your data screen showing sample coverage, export and separate data controls","caption":"The later Your data panel groups coverage and file controls separately from device setup. All values shown are sample data."}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/forma/releases/latest","productActionLabel":"Get Forma for Android","cover":"assets/products/forma.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":6,"productHeaderLabel":"Android release","pdfPage":60,"narrative":{"headline":"A health reading needs [[more than a number]].","scene":"Imagine opening a health dashboard after a watch sync. One measurement is recent, one was last recorded days ago, and another has not been shared. Similar-looking numbers can hide very different situations.","question":"How can a daily overview make the meaning of each reading clear?","insight":"[[Data quality is part of the experience]]. Source, measurement time, missing inputs and model coverage change how a reading should be interpreted.","pivot":"I focused the daily overview on readable measurements, with a clear route into history, source context and score explanations.","takeaway":"Clarity includes knowing what the app cannot tell you.","journey":[{"title":"Orient","detail":"See Today with explicit sample or source context."},{"title":"Inspect","detail":"Open sleep, movement or a measurement history."},{"title":"Understand","detail":"Read the contributors behind an experimental score."},{"title":"Manage","detail":"Use Devices to connect and Your data to inspect coverage or export."}],"decisionLeads":["Keep provenance beside the reading.","Show the inputs behind the score.","Give connection and data tasks separate homes."],"annotations":[{"title":"Native interface","detail":"The Android app uses native screens and charts; the web version is a design preview."},{"title":"Inspectable estimates","detail":"Experimental wellness scores retain input and coverage limits."}],"evidenceTag":"Native artifacts and documented UX audit · sample data in captures","tone":"lime","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Android user reviewing Samsung-shared health records","context":"Moving between a daily summary, a specific measurement and a watch connection that may have partial permissions.","need":"Understand the reading and its source before comparing it with a personal pattern.","friction":"Missing, older and estimated values can look like equally reliable facts.","implication":"Keep time, source and availability visible, with deeper explanation one step away."}},"theme":{"layout":"health-record","mode":"dark","paper":"#101211","ink":"#f4f5ec","dim":"#b4c0b5","line":"#414c43","accent":"#d8fc70","wash":"#303d1e","bodyFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","displayFont":"Plus Jakarta Sans","order":["brief","process","decisions","outcome","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A reading without its context","process":"Treat availability as part of the design","decisions":"From summary to explanation","outcome":"A native overview with inspectable detail","reflection":"An honest limit is useful information"},"decisionHeading":"Keep the context close to the reading.","resultHeading":"A daily overview with a route to the evidence."},"visual":{"presentation":"portrait","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/forma-today.webp","alt":"Archived Forma native Today screen with demo activity rings and sample heart rate","caption":"Archived native Today screen with clearly marked demo values. This capture predates the later Health-tab and personal-tool cleanup.","label":"Archived native interface · sample data","source":"Health++ output/forma-today-rings.png, captured in the project on 13 October 2026. Lossy WebP delivery copy, no UI alteration.","width":1080,"height":2400,"focus":[{"title":"Native interface","detail":"The Android app uses native screens and charts; the web version is a design preview."},{"title":"Inspectable estimates","detail":"Experimental wellness scores retain input and coverage limits."}],"details":[{"decision":0,"x":50,"y":1710,"width":980,"height":430,"alt":"Forma sample heart-rate reading with explicit sample and recorded-range labels","caption":"A detail of the archived Today capture. Sample labels and measurement context stay beside the value."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"ohcaptain","name":"OhCaptain","headline":"Make the next fleet decision clear."}},"ohcaptain":{"slug":"ohcaptain","name":"OhCaptain","kind":"product","headline":"Make the next fleet decision clear.","summary":"A 3-v-3 naval game exploring shared command, role-specific decisions and a combat interface that keeps the sea in view.","role":"Game concept, captain/operator journeys & tactical interface design","status":"Playable browser prototype · keyboard & mouse","context":"Naval capture-and-hold · Operation Blackwater","facts":[{"value":"3 v 3","label":"captain and two operators per crew"},{"value":"8","label":"capture-and-hold relays"},{"value":"10 min","label":"maximum match window"}],"problem":"In OhCaptain, sailing and firing are only part of the job. Each crew shares supplies, but its captain decides how to spend them. Operators need to request help while both roles keep track of a changing battle. I framed the interface around the next decision a player can actually make.","process":["I separated the crew journey into choosing a station, entering solo play or assembling a room, deploying, and managing the battle. A captain still sails and fights; the extra responsibility is distributing shared supplies. Operators request upgrades or repairs rather than spending the treasury themselves.","The documented Clear Deck revision gives combat a compact score/clock, fleet-alive counts, hull status and a persistent map. Spending moves into the Fleet drawer, while the expanded overview holds rosters, recent events and relay details. Warnings compete for a small number of attention slots instead of becoming a wall of banners.","I reviewed the local playable build, documentation and awareness rules. Solo play with AI fill and peer-to-peer rooms are distinct product paths. Online scope depends on a host keeping the page open; the prototype has no dedicated match service or host migration. Rule checks and seeded scenarios do not establish balance or usability with human crews."],"decisions":[{"title":"Make responsibility visible before deployment","choice":"Offer Captain and Vessel Operator as distinct stations. Captains distribute shared supplies through the Fleet drawer; operators use the same crew context to request an upgrade or repair.","reason":"A shared resource needs a clear decision owner. Role-specific actions let people understand why they can request something but cannot buy it directly.","tradeoff":"The captain can become a bottleneck while also sailing and fighting. Pending requests need to remain discoverable without forcing a panel over combat."},{"title":"Keep essential battle state on the field","choice":"Keep the score, clock, relay balance, fleet-alive counts, hull and corner map available. Put spending in a drawer and detailed rosters, events and defenses in the expanded overview.","reason":"The map helps connect local action to the relay objective. Separating routine awareness from deliberate inspection leaves room to aim and navigate.","tradeoff":"An overview can interrupt action. Solo overview pauses the match; online play continues, so players need to understand that distinction. The prototype targets keyboard and mouse rather than claiming a touch-control experience."},{"title":"Give urgent feedback a limited attention budget","choice":"Show at most one off-screen enemy bearing and reserve one text cue for critical hull, safe healing or an endangered home relay. Limit outgoing damage feedback to two numbers and keep routine radio outside combat.","reason":"An alert is useful only if it helps a player choose an action. A priority order makes a received hit or low hull easier to notice among effects, ships and objectives.","tradeoff":"A quieter interface can hide context that an experienced player wants. The map and overview must provide a dependable route to that detail, and the priority policy needs human playtesting."}],"outcome":"The prototype connects role selection, solo AI-filled matches, room-based crew play and a single capture-and-hold arena. Its combat interface separates immediate awareness, spending and tactical inspection. The portfolio captures show the actual local game, rather than presenting the cinematic menu artwork as gameplay. Multi-device reliability, competitive balance and player comprehension remain evaluation work.","reflection":"A fleet game needs coordination as much as combat. The clearest direction was to make responsibility explicit and reduce the number of things demanding attention at once. I would judge the next iteration by whether a new crew understands who can act, what is urgent and why control of a relay matters.","nextSteps":["Proposed playtest: form two crews and ask players to explain their role, request or buy a repair, and identify the relay that matters next without coaching.","Proposed checks: invitation and room entry, role reassignment, a guest disconnecting, restrictive peer-to-peer networks, and an honest explanation when a host leaves.","Proposed observations: missed requests, map-to-world confusion, unnoticed critical alerts and time spent in the overview. Compare these before changing the attention policy."],"sources":[{"label":"OhCaptain README · Play, online limits and Clear Deck interface","detail":"Reviewed 16 October 2026. Captain/operator responsibilities, AI fill, peer-to-peer rooms, eight-relay objectives, ten-minute match window and host/network limitations."},{"label":"src/awareness.js, src/main.js and src/combat.css","detail":"Battle interpretation, role-specific controls, attention priority, local damage feedback and the single off-screen enemy-bearing policy."},{"label":"Local playable build and docs/BLACKWATER-ART.md","detail":"Actual solo combat and expanded-map captures from 16 October 2026. The harbor capture includes the project’s generated cinematic key art; it is menu presentation, distinct from the procedural playable arena."}],"figures":[{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-combat.webp","alt":"OhCaptain actual solo gameplay with compact battle score, hull status and persistent map","caption":"Actual local gameplay with an AI-filled crew: compact battle status, a persistent map, the Fleet control and the player’s hull."},{"src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-map.webp","alt":"OhCaptain expanded tactical map with both fleets, islands and eight labeled relays","caption":"A detail of the actual expanded tactical map. Both fronts, ship positions and relay ownership share the same view; the solo overview pauses play."}],"featured":false,"liveUrl":"https://github.com/srinidhibhat45/OhCaptain","productActionLabel":"View the game on GitHub","cover":"assets/products/ohcaptain.webp","coverKind":"screen","readingMinutes":6,"productHeaderLabel":"GitHub project","pdfPage":63,"narrative":{"headline":"Six vessels. [[One shared decision.]]","scene":"Imagine taking damage while an operator asks for repairs and an enemy crew captures a relay. The captain must decide where shared supplies go while still steering a ship.","question":"What needs to stay visible when sailing, fighting and command happen together?","insight":"[[Information needs a place and a priority]]. An urgent hull warning, a spending decision and a full fleet overview should not demand attention in the same way.","pivot":"I separated immediate combat awareness, role-specific fleet controls and deliberate tactical inspection.","takeaway":"A crew can coordinate when the next action has an owner.","journey":[{"title":"Choose a station","detail":"Understand captain spending or operator requests."},{"title":"Deploy","detail":"Start with AI fill or assemble a crew through a room."},{"title":"Read the battle","detail":"Keep hull, fleet status and the relay map available."},{"title":"Coordinate","detail":"Open Fleet to spend or request; expand the overview to plan."}],"decisionLeads":["Assign responsibility before the battle.","Give each information layer a place.","Let urgent signals take precedence."],"annotations":[{"title":"Keyboard and mouse","detail":"The playable prototype is designed around desktop sailing, aiming and command controls."},{"title":"Peer-to-peer scope","detail":"Rooms rely on a host and direct peer connections; solo AI-filled play remains a separate path."}],"evidenceTag":"Playable prototype and documented Clear Deck revision · solo captures","tone":"navy","audience":{"basis":"Design assumption","label":"Player joining a small naval crew","context":"Learning a captain or operator role while coordinating around a finite relay-control match.","need":"Know who can act, what is urgent and where the next objective is.","friction":"Combat, spending requests and tactical detail can overwhelm the same screen.","implication":"Preserve immediate awareness and reveal deliberate command detail when requested."}},"theme":{"layout":"fleet-command","mode":"dark","paper":"#0b141b","ink":"#e9edf0","dim":"#afc1cc","line":"#405766","accent":"#e7c58c","wash":"#313021","bodyFont":"DM Sans","displayFont":"Archivo","order":["brief","outcome","process","decisions","reflection"],"chapters":{"brief":"A shared treasury in a live battle","process":"Give each information layer a place","decisions":"From combat awareness to command","outcome":"The sea stays in view","reflection":"The next action needs an owner"},"decisionHeading":"Keep combat clear and command within reach.","resultHeading":"One arena, three layers of information."},"visual":{"presentation":"spread","src":"assets/case-study-evidence/ohcaptain-combat.webp","alt":"OhCaptain actual solo gameplay with compact battle score, hull status and persistent map","caption":"Actual local gameplay with an AI-filled crew: compact battle status, a persistent map, the Fleet control and the player’s hull.","label":"Actual solo gameplay","source":"Screenshot of the repository’s local built game on 16 October 2026; WebP delivery copy.","width":1280,"height":720,"focus":[{"title":"Keyboard and mouse","detail":"The playable prototype is designed around desktop sailing, aiming and command controls."},{"title":"Peer-to-peer scope","detail":"Rooms rely on a host and direct peer connections; solo AI-filled play remains a separate path."}],"details":[{"decision":1,"x":14,"y":484,"width":270,"height":222,"alt":"OhCaptain persistent corner map showing relays and fleet positions","caption":"The actual corner map keeps the wider objective available while the player sails."}]},"nextStory":{"slug":"onespace","name":"OneSpace","headline":"Make the next class easier to find."}}};
